y Summer Meeting, June1990", "Theorem1.1 p6; Theorem3.1 pp9–10 and surrounding definitions"], "scope": "Author-hosted lecture text opened. The compact complex surface hypotheses match. The classification theorem is imported as known; its full historical proof is not asserted to have been audited. Publication details beyond the supplied front matter are not inferred.", "reader": "GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, writing run; 17 November 2026 UTC"}]}], "additional_attached_search_record": "deliverables/work/cdp_audit/credit_elsewhere_searches_239.json#C.3.7", "terms_searched": ["bielliptic", "Kawai", "nonexist"], "corpus_size_files": 43, "bounded_absence_statement": "Beyond the explicit positive matches and opened general inputs, no full counterpart was identified in the executed attached-source searches and stated context readings. This is not an exhaustive priority search of all literature.", "existing_scoped_credit_notes": "Kawai1969 Proposition2 entire proof pp605–608 opened; finite-orbit divisor repair retained explicitly."}"}, "historical_source_notes": "SOURCE STATEMENT: Bielliptic general fibres are impossible under b1=b2=0 | SOURCE STATUS: Proved exclusion of bielliptic fibres | SOURCE NOTES: Compact relative Albanese comparison; exceptional classes are vertical, contradicting the positive pullback of a Kähler class on a general bielliptic fibre. Does not use Kawai multiple-fibre corollary. | remarks: All exceptional divisors in the resolution are vertical over omitted base values. Hence every degree-two class on the modified threefold restricts to zero on the general bielliptic fibre, contradicting the positive integral of the Albanese pullback of a Kähler form. The later multiple-fibre corollary of Kawai is not invoked. | proof_sha256: 07e7dead6e864c8b5c056939d46c8e980866e3de8a4021cf89891970f4e2bc00 | Credit evidence (cdp_audit/result_audit.json): Kawai original pp604–606 opened; remaining compactification paragraphs must be read before c