{"row_key": "S6_05_ARITHMETIC:C.4.5", "source_compendium_paper": "I", "source_compendium_label": "ari:C.4.5", "source_file": "C_special.tex C.4.5; code/Cspecial_torsion_numerics.out", "source_line": 5960, "source_section": "Special values, local expansions and torsion", "placement_basis": "candidate body placement; source-credit and proof reviews pending", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Proposition", "source_statement": "\begin{proposition}[complete numerical verification through order eight]\label{ari:C.4.5}
All $13$ smooth exact-order roots for $2\le n\le8$ were evaluated
at $110$ decimal digits independently of the algebraic norm formula.
The discrepancy in $N^{12}$ is below $10^{-90}$ at every root;
the period coordinates also verify $nP=O$ to that tolerance.
There are $0,0,1,2,2,4,4$ roots for $n=2,\ldots,8$.
