{"row_key": "S6_05_ARITHMETIC:C.8.12", "source_compendium_paper": "I", "source_compendium_label": "ari:C.8.12", "source_file": "sections/C_critical.tex", "source_line": 8344, "source_section": "Critical points and the global level curves", "placement_basis": "candidate body placement; source-credit and proof reviews pending", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Proposition", "source_statement": "\begin{proposition}[a radial derivative identity]\label{ari:C.8.12}
Put $p=\wp'_\tau(\mu)$ and
\[
 X=\frac{3Wp}{g_2},\qquad F=1-X,
 \qquad W=\frac{\vartheta_1'(\mu,\tau)}{\vartheta_1(\mu,\tau)}
              +\frac{2\pi i\Im\mu}{\Im\tau}.
\]
These are single-valued modular invariants wherever $g_2\ne0$.
They have continuous extensions at $t=0,1$, with
$X(0)=1/4$ and $X(1)=0$. At every $t\ne0,1$,
\begin{equation}\label{ari:eq:cc-radial}
 (t-1)L_t=\frac{X(X-2)}{24},\qquad
 r\frac{d}{dr}L(1+re^{i\theta})
       =\frac{\Re(X(X-2))}{12}.
\end{equation}
Also, if $a=\Im\mu/\Im\tau$ and $R=\mu'-a$, then
\begin{equation}\label{ari:eq:cc-FR}
 F=\frac{6\pi ip}{g_2}R.
\end{equation}
Consequently $|F|<1$ implies strict decrease along the ray
from $1$ through $t$.
