{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION:lem:triangle-generator-orientations", "source_compendium_paper": "I", "source_compendium_label": "wb:lem:triangle-generator-orientations", "source_file": "workbench/candidate_geometry.tex:32", "source_line": 25292, "source_section": "The analytic threefold constructed in the candidate", "placement_basis": "plan §3 identifies construction/topology appendix re-derivations", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Lemma", "source_statement": "\begin{lemma}[simultaneous elliptic-orientation normalization]
\label{wb:lem:triangle-generator-orientations}
\ The generators may, and henceforth do, satisfy
\begin{equation}
 g_1'(z_1)=e^{-2\pi i/3},\qquad
 g_2'(z_2)=e^{-2\pi i/4}=-i,
\label{wb:eq:triangle-generator-derivatives}
\end{equation}
while \(g_0=(g_1g_2)^{-1}\) remains parabolic.  More precisely, the Cayley
coordinates
\begin{equation}
 s_j(z)=\frac{z-z_j}{z-\bar z_j}
\label{wb:eq:triangle-cayley-coordinates}
\end{equation}
obey
\begin{equation}
 s_1\circ g_1=e^{-2\pi i/3}s_1,
 \qquad
 s_2\circ g_2=-is_2.
\label{wb:eq:triangle-linearizing-identities}
\end{equation}
The two signs in \cref{wb:eq:triangle-generator-derivatives} are a simultaneous
orientation choice; they are not obtained by shrinking a coordinate disc.
