{"row_key": "WORKING_CDP:thm:candidate-conductor", "source_compendium_paper": "II", "source_compendium_label": "wbII:thm:candidate-conductor", "source_file": "workbench/cdp_program.tex", "source_line": 57189, "source_section": "Additional local proofs, analytic duality and Picard restrictions", "placement_basis": "candidate body placement; source-credit and proof reviews pending", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Theorem", "source_statement": "\begin{theorem}[the conductor mechanism for the standard family]
\label{wbII:thm:candidate-conductor}
\ Assume:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(H\arabic*)]
\item $X$ is a connected compact smooth complex threefold and
$f:X\to B$ is a nonconstant holomorphic map to a smooth compact curve;
\item $W=f^{-1}(p_0)$ is the full scheme-theoretic fibre, is reduced, and
has the normal-crossings local models \eqref{wbII:eq:W:5.3};
\item the normalization $\eta:\widetilde W\to W$ is the degree-six del
Pezzo surface $\mathrm{dP}_6$;
\item $H^2(X;\mathbb Z)=0$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, for every $L\in\operatorname{Pic}(X)$,
\[
H^2(W,(T_X\otimes L)|_W)\ne0
\quad\text{and}\quad
R^2f_*(T_X\otimes L)\ne0.
\tag{W.45}\label{wbII:eq:W:5.18}
\]
\end{theorem}", "historical_literature_checks": {"found_in_AC26": "{"status":"found_known_input_or_special_case_in_specified_scope","full_statement_or_conclusion_found":false,"comparison":"The proof uses precisely the normalization-triviality, conductor, Cartier-duality and top-base-change mechanism of AC26. The WB formulation separates four sufficient hypotheses rather than assuming the entire named construction.","positive_or_context_locator":"Theorem10.5(a), proof Steps1–3, pp.88–89","terms_searched":["10.5","conductor","dualising"],"searched_files":[{"path":"sources/original/AC26_transcription.tex","sha256":"9e87e12175c70040ed66ab6513172a1f952c96205601c0910f48712c42445170"},{"path":"sources/external/root/AC26_pdf_text.txt","sha256":"e3a403c0ef610e2aefb22f56b59dd495931dc7015b045cdc30f1403a31a65040"}],"search_record":"deliverables/work/cdp_audit/II_working_credit/searches_150.json#thm:candidate-conductor|AC26","full_search_hits":"deliverables/work/cdp_audit/II_working_credit/search_hits/thm_candidate-conductor__AC26.txt","actual_context_readings":"deliverables/work/cdp_audit/II_working_credit/context_readings.json#AC26","negative_search_limit":"A bounded search and the listed opened contexts do not establish absence from all literature or that every unrelated hit was read."}", "found_in_E26": "{"status":"full_counterpart_not_identified_in_bounded_checked_scope","full_statement_or_conclusion_found":false,"comparison":"No full counterpart to this exact receiving statement was identified in the per-row queries and opened Engel contexts. The construction and invariant-form discussion is not equated with a conductor, duality, or coherent vanishing theorem.","positive_or_context_locator":"Opened §6 invariant-form context and final comparison; see context_readings.json#E26","terms_searched":["10.5","conductor","dualising"],"searched_files":[{"path":"sources/papers/arXiv_2609.38442_Engel_src/S6.tex","sha256":"95ea78327c33351d609a3001539756f0043dfdef13a37a81927608c953c98c3