{"row_key": "CORRECTION_NOTE_20260901:prop:relative-local", "source_compendium_paper": "II", "source_compendium_label": "wbII:prop:relative-local", "source_file": "correction_note/N_singular_fibre_vanishing_step.tex", "source_line": 57654, "source_section": "Additional local proofs, analytic duality and Picard restrictions", "placement_basis": "candidate body placement; source-credit and proof reviews pending", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Proposition", "source_statement": "\begin{proposition}[crossing and multiple-fibre modules]
\label{wbII:prop:relative-local}
The symbols $R_2$ and $R_3$ denote copies of the same regular local ring,
with different maps from the one-dimensional base:
let $R_2=\C\{x,y,z\}$ with base coordinate $t=xy$, and let
$R_3=\C\{x,y,z\}$ with base coordinate $t=xyz$.  Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
 M_2&:=\Omega^1_{R_2/\C\{t\}}
 =\frac{R_2\,dx\oplus R_2\,dy\oplus R_2\,dz}
        {R_2\,(y\,dx+x\,dy)},\\[2pt]
 M_3&:=\Omega^1_{R_3/\C\{t\}}
 =\frac{R_3\,dx\oplus R_3\,dy\oplus R_3\,dz}
        {R_3\,(yz\,dx+xz\,dy+xy\,dz)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{W.176}\label{wbII:eq:CN:6.1}
\]
Both modules are torsion-free, but neither is locally free at the origin.
They embed in free modules by
\[
\begin{aligned}
 M_2&\hookrightarrow R_2e\oplus R_2dz,
 &dx&\longmapsto xe,&dy&\longmapsto-ye,&dz&\longmapsto dz,\\
 M_3&\hookrightarrow R_3e_1\oplus R_3e_2,
 &dx&\longmapsto xe_1,&dy&\longmapsto ye_2,&dz&\longmapsto-z(e_1+e_2),
\end{aligned}
\tag{W.177}\label{wbII:eq:CN:6.2}
\]
with images
\[
 R_2dz\oplus (x,y)e,
 \qquad
 R_3xe_1+R_3ye_2+R_3z(e_1+e_2).
\tag{W.178}\label{wbII:eq:CN:6.3}
\]
If $R_m=\C\{s,y_1,y_2\}$ and the base coordinate is $t=s^m$ with
$m\ge2$, then
\[
\Omega^1_{R_m/\C\{t\}}
\simeq R_m\,dy_1\oplus R_m\,dy_2
       \oplus R_m/(s^{m-1})\,ds,
\tag{W.179}\label{wbII:eq:CN:6.4}
\]
and the final summand is exactly its torsion submodule.
