{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-actual-tube", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-tube", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 63444, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-tube}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-1}

Let \(P\) be a closed oriented smooth six-manifold, and let

\begin{equation}
f:S^3\longrightarrow P
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

be an oriented parametrized embedding. The sphere is the unit sphere in the original ordered quaternion space \(\mathbb H=(1,i,j,k)\), with its boundary orientation. Its oriented rank-three normal bundle is trivial: its hemisphere transition has class in \(\pi_2SO(3)=0\), as proved in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.3} and \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-1} and \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-2}. Retain a chosen normal frame \(\psi_q:\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H\to\nu_{f,q}\), an actual tubular map and a positive coordinate radius \(R\) on which that map is injective.

The left quaternion frame gives the exact bundle isomorphisms

\begin{equation}
TS^3\longleftrightarrow S^3\times\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H,
 \qquad(q,z)\longmapsto(q,\bar qz),\qquad
 (q,h)\longmapsto(q,qh).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.2}
\end{equation}

Here \(\langle q,z\rangle=\operatorname{Re}(\bar qz)=0\). Thus the actual tube can be written as

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 N_R&=\{(q,z)\in\mathbb H\oplus\mathbb H:
                   |q|=1,\ \langle q,z\rangle=0,\ |z|\le R\},\\
 \Gamma(q,z)&=\operatorname{tube}\bigl(f(q),\psi_q(\bar qz)\bigr).
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3}
\end{equation}

The norm on \(z\) is a coordinate norm specifying this auxiliary disc bundle. It does not replace an original ambient metric. If that metric gives the Gram matrix \(G_\psi(q)\) to \(\psi_q\), the actual normal squared length is

\begin{equation}
(\bar qz)^T G_\psi(q)(\bar qz).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.4}
\end{equation}

Both \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3} and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.4} are retained; no metric-isometry assertion about \(\Gamma\) is needed.

Choose actual numbers \(0<a<b<R\). Set

\begin{equation}
\eta(t)=
 \begin{cases}e^{-1/t},&t>0,\\0,&t\le0,\end{cases}
 \qquad
 \beta(r)=\frac{\eta(b-r)}{\eta(b-r)+\eta(r-a)},\qquad
                       \theta(r)=\pi\beta(r).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.5}
\end{equation}

The denominator is positive for every real \(r\), since \(a<b\). The function is smooth, equals one for \(r\le a\), and equals zero for \(r\ge b\). For \(a<r<b\) its derivative is

\begin{equation}
\beta'(r)=
 \frac{-\eta'(b-r)\eta(r-a)-\eta(b-r)\eta'(r-a)}
      {(\eta(b-r)+\eta(r-a))^2}<0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.6}
\end{equation}

Every derivative of \(\eta\) at zero vanishes: on the positive side it is a finite polynomial in \(1/t\) times \(e^{-1/t}\), whose limit is zero. This proves the asserted smoothness at both transition points.

For \(r=|z|>0\), define

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 F_\theta(q,z)&=(q',z'),\\
 q'&=\cos\theta(r)\,q+\frac{\sin\theta(r)}r\,z,\\
 z'&=-r\sin\theta(r)\,q+\cos\theta(r)\,z.
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.7}
\end{equation}

At \(z=0\), define \(F_\theta(q,0)=(-q,0)\). For \(r<a\), \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.7} is exactly \((q,z)\mapsto(-q,-z)\), so this definition is smooth at the entire zero section. For \(r>b\) it is the identity.

The original constraints and radius are preserved. Writing \(c=\cos\theta(r)\), \(s=\sin\theta(r)\), direct expansion gives

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 |q'|^2&=c^2|q|^2+2cs\langle q,z\rangle/r+s^2|z|^2/r^2=1,\\
 \langle q',z'\rangle
      &=-rcs|q|^2+(c^2-s^2)\langle q,z\rangle
                                      +cs|z|^2/r=0,\\
 |z'|^2&=r^2s^2|q|^2-2rcs\langle q,z\rangle+c^2|z|^2=r^2 .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.8}
\end{equation}

Since \(r\) is unchanged, replacing \(\theta\) by \(-\theta\) in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.7} gives the two-sided inverse, by multiplication of the displayed two-by-two rotation matrices. It is smooth at zero for the same reason. Hence \(F_\theta\) is a diffeomorphism of the original \(N_R\).

For clarity, its differential away from zero retains all radial derivatives. With \(\dot r=\langle z,\dot z\rangle/r\), it is

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 \dot q'={}&c\dot q+\frac{s}{r}\dot z
       -s\theta'(r)\dot r\,q
       +\left(\frac{c\theta'(r)}r-\frac{s}{r^2}\right)\dot r\,z,\\
 \dot z'={}&-rs\dot q+c\dot z
       -\bigl(s+rc\theta'(r)\bigr)\dot r\,q
       -s\theta'(r)\dot r\,z .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.9}
\end{equation}

At zero the differential is \((\dot q,\dot z)\mapsto(-\dot q,-\dot z)\) in the surrounding bundle coordinates. The inverse in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.8} proves that this differential is invertible on each tangent space, including at the transition radii. The map preserves orientation: it is the identity on a nonempty outer collar, and its determinant sign is constant on the connected \(N_R\).

Extend the following map by the identity outside the actual tube:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal F_f=
 \begin{cases}
  \Gamma F_\theta\Gamma^{-1},&\text{on }\Gamma(N_R),\\
  1,&\text{on }P\setminus\Gamma(N_R).
 \end{cases}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.10}
\end{equation}

The definitions agree on an open collar because \(b<R\). They give a smooth orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of \(P\), with inverse obtained from \(F_{-\theta}\). On the tube its exact derivative is

\begin{equation}
D\mathcal F_f
       =D\Gamma_{F_\theta(q,z)}\,
             DF_{\theta,(q,z)}\,D\Gamma_{(q,z)}^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.11}
\end{equation}

Thus every chosen tube coordinate, normal frame and original metric comparison remains present.

This model has a precise source comparison. Paul Seidel's original-author TeX, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0105186v2}{\emph{A long exact sequence for symplectic Floer cohomology}, math/0105186v2}, lines 663--730, uses ordered variables \((u,v)\) with \(|v|=1\), \(\langle u,v\rangle=0\), and the flow

\begin{equation}
\sigma_t(u,v)=
  \left(\cos t\,u-\sin t\,|u|v,\
          \cos t\,v+\frac{\sin t}{|u|}u\right).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.12}
\end{equation}

The exact interchange is \((u,v)=(z,q)\). If his function is chosen on \(r\ge0\) as

\begin{equation}
R_{\rm S}(r)=-\frac1{2\pi}\int_r^R\theta(s)\,ds ,
 \qquad 2\pi R_{\rm S}'(r)=\theta(r),
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.13}
\end{equation}

then his formula is exactly \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.7} under that interchange. Near zero \(R_{\rm S}(r)=R_{\rm S}(0)+r/2\); extend it across zero by \(R_{\rm S}(-r)=R_{\rm S}(r)-r\). It is smooth there and vanishes for \(r\ge b\). His retained primitive correction is

\begin{equation}
2\pi\bigl(R_{\rm S}'(r)r-R_{\rm S}(r)\bigr)
              =r\theta(r)+\int_r^R\theta(s)\,ds .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.14}
\end{equation}

We use the smooth map, with its inverse proved above. No ambient symplectic form or symplectic tubular embedding of \(f\) is assumed. The source's ambient extension in lines 979--1002 has an additional Lagrangian hypothesis for its symplectic assertion; \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.10} is a smooth tubular construction under the explicitly proved bundle comparison \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.2}--\eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3}.
