{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-signed-action", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-signed-action", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 63602, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-signed-action}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-2}

Write \(v=f_*[S^3]\in H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\), and write \(\lambda(x,y)=x\cdot y\) for the ordered intersection number using the original orientation. The normal orientation in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3} is the one for which an oriented tangent frame of \(f(S^3)\), followed by its normal frame, is positive in \(P\).

We prove

\begin{equation}
(\mathcal F_f)_*(x)=x-\lambda(v,x)v,
       \qquad
       (\mathcal T_f)_*(x)=x+\lambda(v,x)v,
       \qquad \mathcal T_f=\mathcal F_f^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

This includes the integer sign, rather than only its residue modulo two.

First define the variation map on the original tube:

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 \operatorname{Var}_{F_\theta}:H_3(N_R,\partial N_R;\mathbb Z)
       &\longrightarrow H_3(N_R;\mathbb Z),\\
                         {}[c]&\longmapsto[F_\theta c-c].
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.2}
\end{equation}

A relative cycle has boundary in \(\partial N_R\), on which \(F_\theta\) is the identity, so the displayed difference is a cycle. Replacing \(c\) by \(c+\partial d+e\), where \(e\) is a chain in the boundary, changes that difference by \(\partial(F_\theta d-d)\). This proves that \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.2} is well-defined with the displayed sign.

The oriented Thom comparison from the included duality chapter identifies \(H_3(N_R,\partial N_R;\mathbb Z)\) with \(H_0(S^3;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z\). Its positive generator is the original oriented fibre disc \(D_{q_0}\). Retraction to the zero section identifies \(H_3(N_R;\mathbb Z)\) with \(\mathbb Z[S^3]\). We now calculate \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.2} on \(D_{q_0}\).

Take \(q_0=1\) first, with the ordered fibre coordinates \((i,j,k)\). Projection of its image under \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.7} is

\begin{equation}
z=ru\longmapsto
              \cos\theta(r)\,1+\sin\theta(r)\,u,
       \qquad u\in S^2\subset\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.3}
\end{equation}

It equals \(1\) near the boundary and equals \(-1\) near the centre. It therefore descends to a map \(D^3/\partial D^3\to S^3\). Every target with polar angle \(\vartheta\in(0,\pi)\) has exactly one inverse image: \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.6} gives exactly one \(r\in(a,b)\) with \(\theta(r)=\vartheta\), and the target's unit imaginary direction gives \(u\).

The source orientation is \(dr\wedge\omega_{S^2}\), with its positive radial factor \(r^2\). In the target coordinates \((\vartheta,u)\), the original boundary orientation is \(\sin^2\vartheta\,d\vartheta\wedge\omega_{S^2}\). Indeed the ordered first two columns \((q,\partial_\vartheta q)\) have determinant one in the plane spanned by \(1,u\), and each angular column contributes the positive factor \(\sin\vartheta\). Thus at that unique inverse point the signed Jacobian has the sign of \(\theta'(r)\), namely minus one. Both radial factors are positive. The degree of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.3} is exactly \(-1\).

For arbitrary \(q_0\), left multiplication by \(q_0\) takes this entire calculation to the corresponding fibre. It has determinant \(+1\) in \(\mathbb H\) and takes \((i,j,k)\) to the positive tangent frame \((q_0i,q_0j,q_0k)\). The sign is therefore unchanged. Projection of the original, unmoved fibre is constant, of degree zero. Consequently

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Var}_{F_\theta}[D_{q_0}]
                                      =-[S^3].
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.4}
\end{equation}

The passage from \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.4} to the ambient action has an exact chain description. Subdivide an ambient cycle and cut it across an inner boundary collar of the tube; the resulting inside chain is a relative cycle and the outside chain is fixed by \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.10}. Its variation is precisely \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.2}, included into \(P\). The collar prism produced by the subdivision has the same boundary on both sides and contributes a boundary to the difference. This proves the factorization

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 H_3(P)&\longrightarrow H_3(P,P\setminus\operatorname{int}\Gamma(N_R))
        \xrightarrow[\rm excision]{\ \cong\ }H_3(N_R,\partial N_R)\\
 &\xrightarrow{\operatorname{Var}_{F_\theta}}H_3(N_R)
        \xrightarrow{\Gamma_*}H_3(P)
        \ =\ (\mathcal F_f)_*-1 .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.5}
\end{equation}

Equivalently one may use singular relative chains throughout; excision follows from barycentric subdivision, and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.2} proves independence of every collar-chain choice.

The coefficient of the relative fibre generator in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.5} is \(\lambda(v,x)\). At each transverse intersection, an ordered tangent basis of the zero section followed by an oriented fibre basis is the ambient orientation by our convention. This is exactly the order defining \(\lambda(v,x)\). Summing these local coefficients gives the Thom coefficient, by the included intersection/duality comparison. Equations \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.4}--\eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.5} prove the first formula in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.1}. Since \(\lambda(v,v)=0\) in this skew integral pairing, its inverse is the second formula there. In particular

\begin{equation}
T_v^m(x)=x+m\lambda(v,x)v,\qquad
           m\in\mathbb Z,\qquad T_v=(\mathcal T_f)_* .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.6}
\end{equation}

Expansion verifies

\begin{equation}
\lambda(T_vx,T_vy)
  =\lambda(x,y)+\lambda(v,y)\lambda(x,v)
                     +\lambda(v,x)\lambda(v,y)=\lambda(x,y);
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.7}
\end{equation}

the last possible term is \(\lambda(v,x)\lambda(v,y)\lambda(v,v)=0\). No integral intersection sign has been absorbed into the symbol \(T_v\).

When \(P\) is two-connected and stably framed, every class \(v\in H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\) has an embedded representative by \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-7}. Its normal bundle has the actual trivialization used in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3}. Thus every transvection \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.6} needed below is realized by an actual orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of the original \(P\). The normal frame used to define a twist is not required to extend a framed surgery handle: that extension condition belongs to a different operation. We keep the derivative frame comparison of each twist explicitly in \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-5}.

Illustration description: The retained smooth radial cutoff decreases from pi to zero; a fixed-direction fibre meridian traverses the three-sphere from minus one to plus one, with negative polar degree.

Numerical sample of the exact cutoff \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.5} at the stated auxiliary radii, and one fixed-direction meridian of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.3}. The complete three-dimensional degree is proved using both volume factors in \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-2}; the curve alone is a meridian. Seidel's original source and all comparison factors are identified in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.12}--\eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.14}. Reproducible source: draw\_sphere\_twists.py.
