{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-integral-lagrangians", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-integral-lagrangians", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 63697, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-integral-lagrangians}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-3}

Let \(H\) be a free integral group of rank \(2r\), with a unimodular alternating pairing \(\lambda\). A primitive subgroup \(L\) means that \(H/L\) is torsion-free. An integral Lagrangian is a primitive rank-\(r\) subgroup on which \(\lambda\) vanishes. We prove that a finite product of the transvections \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.6} carries any such \(L\) to any specified integral Lagrangian. All changes below are actual automorphisms of \(H\); the original group and pairing remain fixed.

First construct a symplectic basis adapted to an original Lagrangian \(B\). Choose its basis \(e_1,\ldots,e_r\). Primitivity and the subgroup theorem over \(\mathbb Z\) split \(H=B\oplus B'\). Hence every functional on \(B\) extends to \(H\). Unimodularity then supplies \(y_j\in H\) with

\begin{equation}
\lambda(e_i,y_j)=\delta_{ij}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Write \(C_{jk}=\lambda(y_j,y_k)\), retaining the full skew integer matrix. Define coefficients \(a_{jk}=C_{jk}\) for \(j<k\), and \(a_{jk}=0\) for \(j\ge k\), and put

\begin{equation}
f_j=y_j+\sum_i a_{ij}e_i .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

Every pair then satisfies

\begin{equation}
\lambda(f_j,f_k)=C_{jk}+a_{kj}-a_{jk}=0,\quad
 \lambda(e_i,f_j)=\delta_{ij},\quad
 \lambda(e_i,e_j)=0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.3}
\end{equation}

These classes are an integral basis, not merely a rational basis. To check this, for \(z\in H\) subtract

\begin{equation}
\sum_i\lambda(z,f_i)e_i
                       -\sum_i\lambda(z,e_i)f_i .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.4}
\end{equation}

The remainder pairs to zero with every \(e_i,f_i\). The displayed pairing matrix is invertible over \(\mathbb Q\), so these \(2r\) classes span \(H\otimes\mathbb Q\); the remainder pairs to zero with every element of \(H\). Unimodularity makes it zero. Formula \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.4} is the integral inverse-coordinate map.

In the original coordinates \(z=\sum_i(a_ie_i+b_if_i)\), the twists on one pair act by

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{rcl}
 T_{e_i}^{m}:(a_i,b_i)&\longmapsto&(a_i+mb_i,b_i),\\
 T_{f_i}^{m}:(a_i,b_i)&\longmapsto&(a_i,b_i-ma_i).
 \end{array}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.5}
\end{equation}

They fix all other coordinates. Their explicit product is

\begin{equation}
J_i=T_{e_i}T_{f_i}T_{e_i}:
           (a_i,b_i)\longmapsto(b_i,-a_i),\qquad J_i^2=-I_2.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.6}
\end{equation}

Products act from right to left throughout.

If \(\lambda(u,w)=0\), direct use of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.6} gives

\begin{equation}
E_{u,w}=T_{u+w}T_u^{-1}T_w^{-1},
 \qquad
 E_{u,w}(z)=z+\lambda(u,z)w+\lambda(w,z)u.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.7}
\end{equation}

The added operator has square zero, so its \(m\)-th power adds \(m\) times the same two terms for every integer \(m\). For \(i\ne j\), the original vectors \(u=e_i,w=f_j,u+w=e_i+f_j\) are primitive and perpendicular, and

\begin{equation}
E_{e_i,f_j}^{m}:
      a_i\longmapsto a_i-ma_j,\qquad
      b_j\longmapsto b_j+mb_i ,
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.8}
\end{equation}

with every other coordinate fixed. Formula \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.8} retains the companion change of \(b_j\); it is an automorphism of the entire original symplectic lattice.

Here is a terminating algorithm carrying any primitive \(z\in H\) to \(e_1\).

For each \(i\), integer division in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.5}, followed when needed by \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.6}, performs the Euclidean algorithm on \((a_i,b_i)\). If \(b_i\ne0\), choose the quotient \(q\) with \(a_i=qb_i+s\), \(0\le s<|b_i|\), using a signed divisor if necessary. Apply \(T_{e_i}^{-q}\) to obtain \((s,b_i)\), then \(J_i\) to obtain \((b_i,-s)\). The absolute value of the second nonzero entry strictly decreases. Iteration ends at \((g_i,0)\), where \(g_i\) is a signed greatest common divisor of the two original entries. Use \(J_i^2\) when necessary to make \(g_i\ge0\). This also covers one zero entry, while \((0,0)\) stays zero.

Now every \(b_i\) is zero. Operations \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.8} perform integer row additions on the original \(a\)-coordinates, and keep every \(b_i=0\). Two nonzero \(a\)-entries are reduced by the same division procedure, using row additions and the two-coordinate signed interchange

\begin{equation}
(A,B)\longmapsto(A-B,B)
       \longmapsto(A-B,A)
       \longmapsto(-B,A).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.9}
\end{equation}

Each arrow is a row addition of the form \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.8}, with its full companion \(b\)-action. Their product moves the two coordinates with the displayed signs. A coordinate sign can also be corrected by \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.6}. Thus a finite sequence puts the greatest common divisor of all original \(a_i,b_i\) in the first \(a\)-entry and zero in the others. Primitivity of \(z\) makes that greatest common divisor one: otherwise \(z\) is divisible in \(H\), and conversely a common divisor is exactly that divisibility in an integral basis. The final image is \(e_1\).

We now act on \(L\). Choose a basis element \(x\) of \(L\). It is primitive in \(H\): if \(x=ny\), primitivity of \(L\) first puts \(y\) in \(L\), and primitivity of its basis element forces \(n=\pm1\). The preceding algorithm sends \(x\) to \(e_1\). Call the image Lagrangian \(L_1\). Isotropy gives \(L_1\subset e_1^\perp\), and \(e_1\in L_1\). The actual quotient map and its section are

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 e_1^\perp&=\mathbb Ze_1\oplus H',\qquad
                H'=\bigoplus_{i=2}^r(\mathbb Ze_i\oplus\mathbb Zf_i),\\
 e_1^\perp/\mathbb Ze_1&\longleftrightarrow H',\\
 {}[ce_1+z']&\longmapsto z',\qquad
 z'\longmapsto[z'] .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.10}
\end{equation}

Under these maps \(L_1/\mathbb Ze_1\) is a primitive rank-\((r-1)\) isotropic subgroup of \(H'\). Its primitivity follows because \(e_1^\perp/L_1\) is a subgroup of the torsion-free \(H/L_1\). Its rank and isotropy follow from the displayed direct decomposition and original pairing.

Induct on \(r\). Use only the twists in \(H'\); each fixes both \(e_1\) and \(f_1\) in the full \(H\). They carry that quotient subgroup to \(\operatorname{span}(e_2,\ldots,e_r)\). At this point every lifted generator differs from its \(e_i\) by an integer multiple of \(e_1\), already in \(L_1\); subtracting that multiple proves that the full image is precisely \(B=\operatorname{span}(e_1,\ldots,e_r)\). The induction starts at \(r=0\), with the identity. Every step is a finite product of the displayed twists, so the induction supplies an actual finite product.

In particular a further product of \(J_i^{-1}\), for all \(i\), sends that \(B\) to

\begin{equation}
B^\vee=\operatorname{span}(f_1,\ldots,f_r),
             \qquad J_i^{-1}e_i=f_i.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.11}
\end{equation}

This proves the transitivity needed below, with an algorithm recording every integer quotient, sign and ordered transvection factor. It also proves transitivity on primitive vectors. When \(H=H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\) for the two-connected stably framed \(P\) of \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-2}, replace each factor \(T_v^{m}\) by the exact diffeomorphism \(\mathcal T_f^{m}\) from \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.10} for an embedded representative of that original class \(v\). The ordered composition is a diffeomorphism of the original \(P\) inducing the calculated lattice automorphism. The supporting tubes may intersect; finite composition of the actual maps requires no disjointness among those tubes.
