{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-original-middle-level", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-middle-level", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 63819, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-middle-level}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-4}

Let \(\Sigma\) be the original oriented smooth homotopy six-sphere. Retain its specified stable tangent framing. \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-9} of the \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-original-nullbordism} constructs a compact framed seven-manifold \(W\), together with the actual boundary identification

\begin{equation}
\iota_\Sigma:\Sigma\xrightarrow{\cong}\partial W .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

This is the framed nullbordism supplied by the proved geometric invariant and original integral stable-group calculation. It does not presuppose a smooth identification of \(\Sigma\) with a standard sphere.

Choose an oriented coordinate embedding \(\chi:D^7_\rho\to\operatorname{int}W\), with its actual radius \(\rho>0\), and remove the open coordinate ball. The incoming parametrization is

\begin{equation}
j:S^6\longrightarrow\partial\chi(D^7_\rho),\qquad
                 j(q)=\chi(\rho q),\qquad Dj_q(v)=D\chi_{\rho q}(\rho v).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.2}
\end{equation}

The positive orientation on its domain is the boundary orientation of the coordinate ball. Thus the oriented boundary of the resulting cobordism \(C\) is \(-j(S^6)\sqcup\iota_\Sigma(\Sigma)\). The factor \(\rho\) in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.2} remains part of the map and its differential.

Apply the interior surgeries proved in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8.2} to \(C\), keeping both boundary collars and their maps fixed. They produce a two-connected framed cobordism \(C'\) with precisely these boundaries. The full surgery traces, normal-frame changes and appended stable coordinates are retained by that construction. \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8.3} then gives a handle presentation of this same \(C'\) with only indices three and four. Its proof uses \(H_2(C',S^6)=H_2(C',\Sigma)=0\); it makes no assumption that \(H_3(C')\) is zero or free. The original handle-arrangement proof orders the stages. Write them as

\begin{equation}
C'=C_-\cup_P C_+,\qquad
 C_-:S^6\longrightarrow P,\qquad C_+:P\longrightarrow\Sigma,
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.3}
\end{equation}

with \(r\) index-three handles in \(C_-\) and \(s\) index-four handles in \(C_+\). The common oriented regular level \(P\), its two inclusions, every parameter radius and every full framed attachment are fixed in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.3}. In particular \(P\) is not replaced by a connected-sum model.

The first stage and its reverse have the original relative core models

\begin{equation}
H_k(C_-,S^6;\mathbb Z)=
 \begin{cases}\mathbb Z^r,&k=3,\\0,&k\ne3,\end{cases}
 \qquad
 H_k(C_-,P;\mathbb Z)=
 \begin{cases}\mathbb Z^r,&k=4,\\0,&k\ne4.\end{cases}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.4}
\end{equation}

Consequently the exact sequences, with all endpoint terms specified, give

\begin{equation}
H_4(C_-)=0,\qquad H_3(C_-)\cong\mathbb Z^r,\qquad
 0\longrightarrow\mathbb Z^r
   \xrightarrow{\delta_-}H_3(P;\mathbb Z)
   \xrightarrow{p_-}\mathbb Z^r\longrightarrow0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.5}
\end{equation}

For example \(H_4(S^6)=H_3(S^6)=H_2(S^6)=0\), and the adjacent relative groups in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.4} are zero; these are exactly the zero terms giving \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.5}. The classes \(\delta_-(e_i)\) are the original belt spheres

\begin{equation}
B_i=\{0\}\times S^3_{\beta_i}
 \quad\hbox{of }D^3_{\alpha_i}\times D^4_{\beta_i},
 \qquad b_i=[B_i].
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.6}
\end{equation}

Give each belt the boundary orientation of its original four-disc. The image \(B=\sum_i\mathbb Zb_i\) is primitive because the quotient in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.5} is free. It is isotropic: different belts are disjoint, and a small push in an original \(D^3_{\alpha_i}\)-direction gives a disjoint self-push-off. Equation \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.5} gives \(\operatorname{rank}H_3(P)=2r\).

The original core-cell comparisons also show that \(P\) is two-connected. Attaching only three-cells to \(S^6\) gives \(\pi_1C_-=\pi_2C_-=0\), while \((C_-,P)\) has only four-cells and therefore zero relative homotopy groups through degree three. Its exact sequence identifies \(\pi_1P,\pi_2P\) with those of \(C_-\). The stable frame on \(C'\) supplies one on \(P\). The integral intersection pairing

\begin{equation}
\lambda:H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\times H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\longrightarrow\mathbb Z
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.7}
\end{equation}

is alternating and unimodular by the full integral duality comparison in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-6.3}. In particular \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.6} spans an integral Lagrangian.

Read the second stage in reverse. It consists of \(s\) index-three handles from the original \(\Sigma\) to the same underlying \(P\). Since \(\Sigma\) has the integral homology and low homotopy groups of \(S^6\), the same relative argument gives

\begin{equation}
0\longrightarrow\mathbb Z^s
      \xrightarrow{\delta_+}H_3(P;\mathbb Z)
      \xrightarrow{p_+}\mathbb Z^s\longrightarrow0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.8}
\end{equation}

The orientation on the reversed middle boundary is the opposite one if the reversed cobordism is given the opposite orientation; its intersection pairing is then \(-\lambda\). Both primitivity and isotropy are unchanged by this specified sign. In coordinates an original upper handle \(D^4_{\gamma_j}\times D^3_{\delta_j}\), with coordinates \((x,y)\), reverses to the handle with ordered coordinates \((y,x)\). The block permutation has sign \((-1)^{4\cdot3}=+1\); a constant reflection used for the chosen reversed orientation is recorded separately. The reversed belt \(\{0\}_y\times S^3_{\gamma_j,x}\) is exactly the original upper attaching sphere. Its orientation differs, if at all, by that recorded constant sign.

Let \(a_j\) be the class of the original upper attaching sphere with the boundary orientation of its original \(x\)-core. Thus \(\delta_+(e_j)=\varepsilon_j a_j\), with the explicit reversal sign \(\varepsilon_j\in\{1,-1\}\). Equation \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.8} proves that

\begin{equation}
L=\sum_{j=1}^s\mathbb Za_j\quad\hbox{is primitive and isotropic},
 \qquad 2s=\operatorname{rank}H_3(P)=2r.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.9}
\end{equation}

It follows that \(s=r\) and \(L\) is an integral Lagrangian. This conclusion does not assert that \(B\) and \(L\) were complementary in the original filling.

Retain the original signed matrix as well. Let \(\sigma_i\) be the orientation sign of the ordered lower handle coordinates \((u_1,u_2,u_3,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4)\) relative to \(C'\). Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.4} of the geometric framing chapter, with its ordered tangent calculation, gives the original handle boundary matrix

\begin{equation}
D^{\rm old}_{ij}=\sigma_i\lambda(b_i,a_j),\qquad
 H_4(C',S^6)=\ker D^{\rm old},\quad
 H_3(C',S^6)=\operatorname{coker}D^{\rm old}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.10}
\end{equation}

Indeed the relative boundary of the \(j\)-th original four-core is its oriented attaching sphere. The projection \(r_3p_-\) in that equation gives its coordinates in the original lower core basis, and multiplication by \(\sigma_i\) is its own inverse. There are no other relative handle groups. Thus every free or torsion contribution of the old matrix remains visible in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.10}.

Apply \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-3} to \(H=H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\) with \(e_i=b_i\). Construct the actual dual basis \(f_i\), including its full skew correction \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.2}, and use the finite twist algorithm to obtain an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism

\begin{equation}
\phi:P\longrightarrow P,\qquad
             \phi_*L=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb Z}(f_1,\ldots,f_r).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.11}
\end{equation}

Every class used in this algorithm is represented by an embedded three-sphere by \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-7} of the geometric framing chapter; its rank-three normal bundle is trivial. \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-1} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-2} supply each actual tube diffeomorphism. This verifies the geometric realization of every algebraic factor. If \(r=0\), take \(\phi=1_P\); there are already no handles.
