{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-original-gluing", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-gluing", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 63936, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-gluing}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-5}

Write \(i_-:P\to\partial_+C_-\) and \(i_+:P\to\partial_-C_+\) for the inclusions in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.3}. Their original gluing identifies \(i_+(p)\) with \(i_-(p)\). Define a new cobordism by the actual equivalence relation

\begin{equation}
C_\phi=(C_-\sqcup C_+)/\bigl(i_+(p)\sim i_-(\phi(p))\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

The two outer boundary maps \(j\) and \(\iota_\Sigma\) are retained literally. The natural maps of each piece into \(C'\) and \(C_\phi\) identify the interiors and their outer collars. They do not claim an identification of the two entire fillings; their old and new middle homology may differ.

Here is the smooth seam. Retain original collar parameters

\begin{equation}
c_-:P\times(-d_-,0]\to C_-,\qquad
 c_+:P\times[0,d_+)\to C_+,
 \qquad d_-,d_+>0,
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

directed from the lower to the upper piece. Restrict to \(d=\min(d_-,d_+)\), without changing either parameter scale. A chart neighbourhood of the seam is parametrized by

\begin{equation}
\Xi_\phi(q,t)=
 \begin{cases}
    c_-(q,t),&-d<t\le0,\\
    c_+(\phi^{-1}(q),t),&0\le t<d .
 \end{cases}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.3}
\end{equation}

At \(t=0\) these are the same quotient point by \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.1}. Together with the original interior charts, \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.3} defines a smooth atlas: on either open half its transition maps are the original collar maps composed with the smooth diffeomorphism \(\phi^{-1}\). The inverse coordinate is \((q,t)\) on the lower half and \((\phi(p),t)\) on the upper half. The two pieces are compact and are glued along a closed boundary by a homeomorphism; the quotient is Hausdorff (disjoint compact equivalence classes have disjoint saturated collar neighbourhoods) and compact. This establishes the underlying manifold structure, not merely a formal gluing notation.

In upper coordinates \((p,t)\) the tangent identification at the seam, written in the ordered summands \(TP\oplus\mathbb R_t\), is

\begin{equation}
(v,a)\longmapsto(D\phi_pv,a).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.4}
\end{equation}

The orientation of \(\phi\) is positive, and \(t\) keeps its direction. Hence the given orientations on the two original pieces extend to the stated oriented cobordism from \(S^6\) to \(\Sigma\).

To retain the full stable-frame comparison, append identity coordinates until the two original piece frames have a common rank \(7+h\). Express them through \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.2} as isomorphisms

\begin{equation}
\tau_{\pm,p}:T_pP\oplus\mathbb R_t\oplus\mathbb R^h
                                    \longrightarrow\mathbb R^{7+h}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.5}
\end{equation}

If an original boundary frame was given using an outward rather than a directed collar vector, the corresponding sign is included in \(\tau_{\pm,p}\). The old and new comparisons are exactly

\begin{equation}
A_{\rm old}(p)=\tau_{-,p}\tau_{+,p}^{-1},\qquad
 A_\phi(p)=\tau_{-,\phi(p)}
              (D\phi_p\oplus1\oplus I_h)\tau_{+,p}^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.6}
\end{equation}

The original global frame supplies the old seam compatibility or its specified collar homotopy. The new map \(A_\phi\) is retained as an actual matrix-valued map. Its derivative factor is the ordered product of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.11} for the chosen twist factors, evaluated at their successive image points. No null-homotopy of \(A_\phi\), and no preservation of the original frame by \(\phi\), is being asserted. Equation \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.6} is the exact comparison between the two framed pieces in the new oriented smooth filling.

Likewise the full upper attaching tube, originally

\begin{equation}
\alpha_j:S^3_{\gamma_j}\times D^3_{\delta_j}\longrightarrow P,
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.7}
\end{equation}

becomes \(\phi\circ\alpha_j\) in the lower seam coordinates. Its differential is \(D\phi_{\alpha_j(q,z)}D\alpha_{j,(q,z)}\), including every tangent and normal column. A normal bundle here is first the quotient by the attaching tangent bundle; this derivative induces the exact quotient isomorphism and carries its frame. If one uses metric-orthogonal normal vectors, apply the original metric projection to these columns and retain that projection map. Invertibility on the quotient guarantees that they still form a frame. Neither \(\gamma_j\), \(\delta_j\), nor any derivative factor has been removed.

There is no requirement for a global stable framing on \(C_\phi\) in the subsequent smooth cancellation theorem. Its data are oriented full handle attachments, which \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.7} has supplied. The original stable frame was used to construct \(W,C'\), to prove the properties of \(P\), and to supply the embedded representatives. Those uses are complete before the regluing. The original frame and its precise defect \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.6} remain part of the comparison.
