{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-actual-cancellation", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-cancellation", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 64011, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-cancellation}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-6}

Define \(F_i=\sigma_i f_i\). The classes \(F_i\) are still a basis of the complementary Lagrangian. Since \(\phi_*\) maps the basis \(a_j\) of \(L\) onto another basis of this same subgroup, there is a unique unimodular integer matrix \(U\) with

\begin{equation}
\phi_*a_j=\sum_iF_iU_{ij},\qquad
        U_{ij}=\sigma_i\lambda(b_i,\phi_*a_j).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

The equality on the right follows from \(\lambda(b_i,f_k)=\delta_{ik}\) and \(\sigma_i^2=1\). Its determinant is \(1\) or \(-1\) because both displayed collections are bases of the same free group.

The full relative handle chain complex of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.1}, in its original lower and transported upper core bases, is

\begin{equation}
0\longrightarrow\mathbb Z^r
                \xrightarrow{\,D^\phi=U\,}\mathbb Z^r
                       \longrightarrow0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.2}
\end{equation}

This is the same signed core-to-belt calculation that gave \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.10}, now applied to the actual tubes \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.7}. Both matrices \(D^{\rm old}\) and \(D^\phi\) are retained; \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.2} does not erase the kernel or torsion cokernel of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.10}. It proves that the new relative groups vanish because \(U\) is an integer isomorphism.

For actual cancellation, factor \(U^{-1}\) into the finite integer additions, label permutations and signed basis comparisons proved in \cref{courseIII:higher-index-framed-slides-and-chain-maps:section-6} of the \cref{courseIII:higher-index-framed-slides-and-chain-maps:higher-slide-integer-changes}. Perform each addition by its full index-four framed slide in the seven-dimensional cobordism. The hypotheses there are satisfied: the lower boundary \(P\) is connected, and \(2\le4\le7-2\). Carry the full tubes and outgoing boundary map through its specified cobordism diffeomorphism after every operation. Its signed chain-prism proof gives the exact comparison

\begin{equation}
D_{\rm comparison}^{\rm new}=U\,U^{-1}=I_r .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.3}
\end{equation}

There is no index-five boundary to omit; its adjacent source group is zero. The lower index-three basis is kept fixed.

It is essential to retain what a signed basis change means geometrically. The slide proof leaves every original oriented core identifiable. After all the actual slides and permutations, let \(c_j^{\rm geom}\) be the resulting upper geometric core generator. The final comparison basis is \(\widehat c_j=\epsilon_j c_j^{\rm geom}\), with each recorded \(\epsilon_j\in\{1,-1\}\); assigning this sign does not assert an orientation-reversing ambient isotopy. The lower geometric core bases were fixed. Therefore \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.3} is equivalent to the full geometric identities

\begin{equation}
D^{\rm geom}_{ij}=\epsilon_j\delta_{ij},\qquad
 [A_j^{\rm geom}]=\epsilon_jF_j,\qquad
       \lambda(b_i,[A_j^{\rm geom}])
                             =\epsilon_j\sigma_i\delta_{ij}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.4}
\end{equation}

To verify the second identity, the upper comparison generator is the recorded linear combination \(U^{-1}\) of the transported original generators; its attaching homology class is \(\sum_k\phi_*a_k(U^{-1})_{kj}=F_j\). Multiply by \(\epsilon_j\) to recover its original geometric orientation. This proves every sign in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.4}.

Perturb the disjoint upper attaching spheres and their full tubes slightly to be transverse to the lower belts, retaining their parameter domains and frames by the derivative of the perturbing ambient isotopy. The finite-chart transverse perturbation in the receiving Whitney chapter supplies such an isotopy. All intersections are then finite. For each pair \(A_j^{\rm geom},B_i\), \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.4} gives algebraic count zero off the diagonal and absolute count one on the diagonal. Pair all opposite-sign points. After each removal, retain the single point of sign \(\epsilon_j\sigma_j\) on the diagonal.

Every such removal has the precise hypotheses of the \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:receiving-whitney-levels}. The level before the original index-three stage is \(S^6\) with its retained product collar, so it is simply connected. The sheet dimensions are exactly \((d,p,q)=(6,3,3)\). The original belt complement is diffeomorphic to that \(S^6\) minus its disjoint attaching two-sphere cores; a loop and a null-homotopy can avoid them because \(1+2<6\) and \(2+2<6\). The exact complement diffeomorphism and all its radial coefficients are those of that included construction.

The two arcs joining a chosen opposite-sign pair give its Whitney circle. The disk filling, with its complete collar fixed, can avoid every other three-dimensional sheet because \(2+3<6\), and can be embedded because \(2+2<6\). The rank-four adapted normal-frame calculation of \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-3} removes its actual rotation obstruction. Its \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-4} then supplies the supported ambient flow, the derivative transport of every upper normal column, and the full diffeomorphism of attached cobordisms. Its support misses all other sheets except the two prescribed patches. Thus it removes exactly the chosen two points and creates none.

There are finitely many points and each operation decreases their number by two. Finite iteration gives

\begin{equation}
\#(A_j^{\rm geom}\cap B_i)=\delta_{ij}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.5}
\end{equation}

The remaining diagonal signs are still those in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.4}. This geometric assertion follows after the supported moves; it is stronger than the algebraic equation \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.3}.

Apply \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:section-5} of the \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:middle-actual-incidence} to this complete modified presentation. Here three is the lowest remaining index, the level below it is the incoming product, the \(r\) lower points have one common value, and the \(r\) upper points have another strictly larger value. That proof builds the actual function and descending field from all the retained handle maps and parameter radii. Its intersection-to-trajectory bijection turns \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.5} into exactly one transverse descending trajectory from the \(j\)-th upper point to the \(j\)-th lower point, and no such trajectory to any other lower point.

For completeness, its escape and support hypotheses can be checked directly in this presentation. Fix a regular value \(a_0\) in the incoming product below all lower points. A descending trajectory which does not reach \(a_0\) stays in a compact band and must limit to a critical point: at a noncritical limit point the strictly negative derivative would give a uniform positive drop on infinitely many disjoint time intervals, contradicting the lower bound on the function. The connected limit set is contained in the finite critical set, hence is one point. The common upper value excludes every other upper point, and the trajectory bijection excludes every lower point except the one on the specified trajectory. Thus all other trajectories escape to \(a_0\).

Near that lower point the actual quadratic coordinates retain

\begin{equation}
f=c-\sum_{\nu=1}^3 a_\nu x_\nu^2+
                     \sum_{\mu=1}^4 b_\mu y_\mu^2,\qquad
 x(t)=e^t x(0),\quad y(t)=e^{-t}y(0),\qquad a_\nu,b_\mu>0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.6}
\end{equation}

These formulas show that the only additional limits of the upper unstable sheet are in the chosen lower unstable disc. The compact lower exit sphere reaches \(a_0\) in uniformly bounded time, by a finite cover of its transverse hitting neighbourhoods. The same holds for the compact remainder of the upper attaching sphere away from the chosen intersection. The resulting truncated unstable closure is therefore compact, contains only this pair of critical points, and stays away from both boundary collars. Distinct pairs have disjoint such compact sets by uniqueness of trajectories and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.5}. Choose disjoint neighbourhoods with compact closure around them. These are exactly the supported cancellation hypotheses proved in that chapter.

Its cancellation formula now removes every index-three/four pair, with the retained signs permitted by its theorem. All comparison diffeomorphisms and boundary maps are carried through the finite sequence. No critical point remains. The resulting function \(f_{\rm end}\) on the actual \(C_\phi\), viewed through those comparisons, has original constant boundary values \(\ell_-<\ell_+\) and no critical point.

Choose a smooth positive transverse field \(V\) matching its prescribed boundary collars, and retain the full multiplier in

\begin{equation}
Y=\frac{V}{df_{\rm end}(V)},\qquad df_{\rm end}(Y)=1,\qquad
                        T=\ell_+-\ell_->0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.7}
\end{equation}

The actual product map and its inverse are

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \Psi:S^6\times[0,T]&\longrightarrow C_\phi,
                  &\Psi(q,t)&=\operatorname{Fl}^{Y}_t(j(q)),\\
 \Psi^{-1}(w)&=
 \left(j^{-1}\operatorname{Fl}^{Y}_{-(f_{\rm end}(w)-\ell_-)}(w),
                          f_{\rm end}(w)-\ell_-\right).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.8}
\end{equation}

On each trajectory the function increases by exactly its time, so compactness gives existence up to both specified endpoints and each intermediate level is crossed once. This proves both inverse identities. The derivative in a tangent direction is \(D\operatorname{Fl}^Y_t\,Dj\), and the time column is \(Y\), transverse to the level. Thus both maps are smooth with invertible differentials, including in their boundary collars. All earlier presentation comparisons are part of \(j\) and \(\iota_\Sigma\) in this formula. If one compares with the interval \([0,1]\), the map is \((q,s)\mapsto\Psi(q,Ts)\), whose second derivative column is \(T Y\); the original positive length \(T\) is retained.
