{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-original-smooth-map", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 64113, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-7}

Restrict the product map to its outgoing boundary and use the original boundary identification:

\begin{equation}
d:S^6\longrightarrow\Sigma,\qquad
              d(q)=\iota_\Sigma^{-1}\bigl(\Psi(q,T)\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Every factor in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1} is a specified smooth map and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.8} gives its smooth inverse:

\begin{equation}
d^{-1}(x)=j^{-1}\operatorname{Fl}^{Y}_{-T}(\iota_\Sigma(x)).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

It is orientation-preserving. The positive orientation on the incoming level was specified by the disc in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.2}. The directed product orientation has boundary \(-S^6\) at \(0\) and \(+\Sigma\) at \(T\). The flow preserves the level orientation since its transverse time column stays positive. All intervening comparisons are orientation-preserving. Hence \(d_*[S^6]=[\Sigma]\), with the original orientations, without an unrecorded reflection.

Take now \(\Sigma=X\), the constructed compact smooth manifold. Its original degree-one map \(g:S^6\to X\) was constructed in Paper~I, Theorem~I.1. The included first Hurewicz theorem gives an isomorphism

\begin{equation}
h_6:\pi_6(X,x_*)\xrightarrow{\cong}H_6(X;\mathbb Z),
                         [u]\longmapsto u_*[S^6].
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.3}
\end{equation}

The hypothesis is verified there: \(X\) is five-connected by its integral homology calculation and successive first Hurewicz applications. If the basepoint of \(d\) differs from that of \(g\), choose a path between their images and use the homotopy-extension property of \(\{q_*\}\subset S^6\) to move the basepoint along that path. The induced based class is independent of the path, since \(\pi_1X=0\). Both \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1} and \(g\) map under \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.3} to the same generator \([X]\). Injectivity supplies a based homotopy after that basepoint change, and adjoining the basepoint-change homotopy gives the actual free homotopy

\begin{equation}
d\simeq g .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

This uses equality of the original oriented degree classes, not a new choice of the original map.

The homotopy inverse retained in lesson 7 is \(h_X:X\to S^6\). In this companion write \(h=h_X\), with no change to that original map. Compose \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.4} with \(h\) to get \(hd\simeq hg\simeq1_{S^6}\); precompose this homotopy with \(d^{-1}\). It gives

\begin{equation}
h\simeq d^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.5}
\end{equation}

Thus both original homotopy-equivalence maps receive a proved comparison to inverse smooth diffeomorphisms.

The previous tangent comparison is retained as well. Let \(B:g^*TX\to TS^6\) be the rank-six bundle isomorphism of the spin-six chapter, \eqref{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:eq:6.3}, and let \(H(q,s)\) be the homotopy from \(g\) to \(d\) just obtained. The finite projection transport of \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-10.1} constructs an actual oriented isomorphism \(P_H:g^*TX\to d^*TX\): embed the bundle over the compact homotopy cylinder in a trivial bundle, partition its parameter interval finely enough that adjacent projection operators differ in norm by less than one, and compose their restricted projection isomorphisms. Their inverse operators, in reverse order, give its inverse. Compactness makes one finite partition work for every \(q\). This uses the original continuous homotopy and needs no smoothness assumption on \(g\). The exact automorphism comparing the old bundle identification with the differential of the new map is

\begin{equation}
E_q=B_q(P_H)_q^{-1}Dd_q:T_qS^6\longrightarrow T_qS^6 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.6}
\end{equation}

It is invertible and orientation-preserving, and the exact relation is

\begin{equation}
Dd_q=(P_H)_qB_q^{-1}E_q .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.7}
\end{equation}

No claim that \(E\) is the identity or nullhomotopic is needed or made. Stabilizing retains the original radial coordinate: the differential-induced frame of \(d^*TX\oplus\mathbb R\) sends \((v,a)\) to \((Dd_q)^{-1}v+a q\), with inverse \(w\mapsto(Dd_q(w-\langle w,q\rangle q),\langle w,q\rangle)\). The earlier \(B\)-frame, transported through \(P_H\), differs on the tangent summand by exactly \(E_q\) and on the radial line by the identity. This records the full rank-six and rank-seven comparisons.

The same proof applies to every oriented smooth homotopy six-sphere. Its actual bundle \(T\Sigma\oplus\mathbb R\) is trivial by \(\pi_5SO(7)=0\) and the pullback/descent construction proved in \cref{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:section-6} of the spin-six chapter. The stable tangent-to-normal framing comparison needed for the whole framed bordism group is proved in \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-7.1} below, extending the original complex-case comparison without changing its endpoints. The geometric framing proof therefore gives a nullbordism for this actual stable tangent frame because its middle homology is zero, so its proved quadratic invariant has value zero. \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-6} above then apply without a complex structure. Consequently every such sphere admits the orientation-preserving diffeomorphism \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1}. In particular the group of oriented smooth homotopy six-spheres is

\begin{equation}
\Theta_6=0 .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.8}
\end{equation}

This is the classical smooth recognition result, proved here with its receiving maps; no novelty is asserted.

One can also retain a smooth filling. Cap the incoming boundary of \(C_\phi\) by the original coordinate seven-disc using \(j\). Equation \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.8} identifies the attached collar with its actual product; extending a disc by a finite collar is diffeomorphic to a disc by a smooth increasing radial coordinate whose derivative stays positive, equal to the original coordinate near the centre. Such a function is obtained by integrating a positive smooth function with the prescribed constant endpoint germs and the required total radial length. The full radial derivative and the original factor \(\rho\) specify that comparison. This gives a smooth disc filling of the underlying original \(\Sigma\). It does not claim that the new filling's framing agrees with the two old piece frames; their exact difference is \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.6}. The original framed nullbordism remains retained separately.
