{"row_key": "course_CG-S6:twist-general-stable-framing", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-general-stable-framing", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison.md", "source_line": 64188, "source_section": "Sphere twists and the original smooth six-sphere comparison", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "numbered proof section", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-general-stable-framing}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-7.1}

This argument supplies an additional receiving prerequisite used by the general framed-bordism calculation. It is a direct bundle calculation from the original Pontryagin--Thom matrices, independent of the gluing and smooth-recognition conclusions above, so it may be used before \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-4}. \cref{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:section-8} of the Pontryagin--Thom companion proved a homotopy for the original complex tangent bundle. Its equation \eqref{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.11} also calculated the exact comparison for an arbitrary stable tangent frame. We now prove that this latter comparison is stably homotopic to the identity in dimension six, retaining every original map and using no complex structure on the original tangent bundle.

Use that chapter's notation: \(J:T_mM\to\mathbb R^N\) is the derivative of the actual embedding, \(H=J^TJ\) is its Gram matrix, \(Q=H^{-1}J^T\), and \(P_\nu=I-JQ\). The actual stable tangent frame is

\begin{equation}
\tau:T M\oplus\mathbb R^k\xrightarrow{\cong}\mathbb R^{6+k},
 \qquad QJ=I,\quad QP_\nu=0,\quad P_\nu+JQ=I_N .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.9}
\end{equation}

The notation \(H\) in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.9} is this original Gram matrix, distinct from the earlier map homotopy \(H(q,s)\). If the original \(k\) is odd, append one identity coordinate to \(\tau\), in the original last-coordinate convention. Denote the resulting even number again by \(k\), recording this one stabilization explicitly. If it is already even, append none. In particular the original rank-seven frame of a homotopy six-sphere uses exactly one further coordinate here.

Give the ordered trivial spaces \(\mathbb R^{6+k}\) and \(\mathbb R^k\) their constant operators \(J_{6+k}\) and \(J_k\), acting on each successive coordinate pair by \((x,y)\mapsto(-y,x)\). For \(k=0\) the second operator and its formulas act on the zero space. The original \(\tau\) determines the operator

\begin{equation}
\mathcal I=\tau^{-1}J_{6+k}\tau
        \quad\hbox{on }E=TM\oplus\mathbb R^k,\qquad
 \mathcal I^2=-I_E .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.10}
\end{equation}

This is an operator on the specified stabilized bundle; it makes no claim that \(TM\) alone has such an operator.

For \(0\le t\le1\), put \(\alpha=\pi t\), \(\theta=\pi t/2\), and define

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 C_t&=\cos\alpha\,I_E+\sin\alpha\,\mathcal I,\\
 R_t&=I_{TM}\oplus(\cos\alpha\,I_k+\sin\alpha\,J_k),\\
 D_t&=R_tC_t .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.11}
\end{equation}

Their exact inverses replace each sine by its negative and reverse the product:

\begin{equation}
C_t^{-1}=\cos\alpha\,I_E-\sin\alpha\,\mathcal I,\qquad
 R_t^{-1}=I_{TM}\oplus(\cos\alpha\,I_k-\sin\alpha\,J_k),\qquad
 D_t^{-1}=C_t^{-1}R_t^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.12}
\end{equation}

Multiplication, using the two squared-operator identities, proves both inverse identities in every fibre. The endpoints are

\begin{equation}
D_0=I_E,\qquad D_1=(-I_{TM})\oplus I_k .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.13}
\end{equation}

Indeed \(C_1=-I_E\) and \(R_1=I_{TM}\oplus(-I_k)\), so the auxiliary minus signs cancel exactly.

For the original variables \((v,a,b)\in TM\oplus\mathbb R^N\oplus\mathbb R^k\), put \(u=Qa\) and \(y=P_\nu a\). Define the full homotopy by

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 v_t&=\cos\theta\,v+\sin\theta\,u,\\
 (w_t,b_t)&=D_t(-\sin\theta\,v+\cos\theta\,u,\ b),\\
 a_t&=y+Jw_t,\qquad
 \mathcal S_t(v,a,b)=(v_t,a_t,b_t).
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.14}
\end{equation}

Here every occurrence of \(\tau,J,H,Q,P_\nu\) is the original point-dependent map from \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.9}; none has been replaced by an orthonormal constant matrix.

The inverse is explicit. Given \((v_t,a_t,b_t)\), recover \(y=P_\nu a_t\), \(w_t=Qa_t\), then put \((w,b)=D_t^{-1}(w_t,b_t)\). Finally set

\begin{equation}
v=\cos\theta\,v_t-\sin\theta\,w,\qquad
 u=\sin\theta\,v_t+\cos\theta\,w,\qquad a=y+Ju .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.15}
\end{equation}

Equations \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.9} and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.12} prove both composites are the identity. Thus \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.14} is a homotopy through bundle isomorphisms. Its endpoints are

\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_0=I,\qquad
 \mathcal S_1(v,a,b)=(Qa,\ P_\nu a+Jv,\ b).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.16}
\end{equation}

The second is precisely the original involution \(S_m\) in equation \eqref{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.11} of the Pontryagin--Thom chapter, with the stated appended coordinate if needed.

All derivative terms are retained as well. In any original bundle chart,

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 d\mathcal I
   &=-\tau^{-1}(d\tau)\tau^{-1}J_{6+k}\tau
                           +\tau^{-1}J_{6+k}\,d\tau,\\
 dC_t
   &=(-\sin\alpha\,I_E+\cos\alpha\,\mathcal I)\,d\alpha
                                      +\sin\alpha\,d\mathcal I,\\
 dR_t
   &=0_{TM}\oplus(-\sin\alpha\,I_k+\cos\alpha\,J_k)\,d\alpha,\\
 dD_t&=(dR_t)C_t+R_t\,dC_t .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.17}
\end{equation}

In \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.14}, \(du=(dQ)a+Q\,da\) and \(dy=(dP_\nu)a+P_\nu\,da\). Writing \(z=-\sin\theta\,v+\cos\theta\,u\), its full remaining derivatives are

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 dv_t={}&\cos\theta\,dv+\sin\theta\,du
                  +(-\sin\theta\,v+\cos\theta\,u)d\theta,\\
 dz={}&-\sin\theta\,dv+\cos\theta\,du
                  +(-\cos\theta\,v-\sin\theta\,u)d\theta,\\
 d(w_t,b_t)={}&(dD_t)(z,b)+D_t(dz,db),\\
 da_t={}&dy+(dJ)w_t+J\,dw_t .
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.18}
\end{equation}

Here \(dQ=-H^{-1}(dH)H^{-1}J^T+H^{-1}dJ^T\), \(dH=(dJ)^TJ+J^TdJ\), and \(dP_\nu=-(dJ)Q-J\,dQ\), by differentiating \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.9}. These formulas verify smooth dependence while preserving the entire metric comparison.

The earlier exact equality is \(\tau_E=\tau_{\rm st}S_m\). Equations \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.14}--\eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.16} therefore give the required general homotopy

\begin{equation}
\tau_{\rm st}\mathcal S_t:
                         \tau_{\rm st}\simeq\tau_E .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.19}
\end{equation}

It applies to every stably framed six-manifold and to a smooth family of its frames. Making its parameter constant near the endpoints lets it be installed in a boundary collar exactly as in (8.15)--(8.16) of the Pontryagin--Thom chapter. The original boundary map and specified stable frame are then recovered.

This completes the tangent-framed use of the normal-framed Pontryagin--Thom bijection for the entire six-dimensional bordism group used in the geometric framing chapter, (9.1). One direction first forms the original normal frame by that chapter's explicit embedding and normal-bundle map; the inverse first takes a regular inverse image and its tangent-plus-normal frame. Their composite comparison is precisely \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.19}. For a bordism, extend the prescribed boundary embedding over a collar and use the relative embedding and frame constructions from the same chapter; \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.19} corrects its boundary frame while fixing the rest of the collar. Conversely a normal-framed bordism has the tangent frame \eqref{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.15}, and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.19} recovers its prescribed endpoint frames. Thus the same explicit comparisons give injectivity, surjectivity and compatibility with bordism, without an unproved complex-structure hypothesis on an arbitrary representative.

Illustration description: The original lower and upper seven-dimensional pieces are glued through phi on their common level; both old and new incidence matrices and the full frame defect are retained before supported cancellation produces the actual product and outgoing diffeomorphism.

Schematic of the exact maps \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.3}--\eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.5}, with the original outer boundary parametrizations fixed. The boxes represent the two original pieces; they do not assert a geometric embedding of a seven-manifold in the page. Both the torsion-bearing old matrix and the new frame defect are retained. The full smooth argument is in \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-6} and \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-7}. Reproducible source: draw\_sphere\_twists.py.
