{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-PROJECTIVE-5", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:projective-splitting-for-lesson11:CG-S6-PROJECTIVE-5", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/projective-splitting-for-lesson11.md", "source_line": 42711, "source_section": "Integral projective splitting", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical labelled complete classical derivation", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:projective-splitting-for-lesson11:CG-S6-PROJECTIVE-5}

Define \(c_1(L)=e(L_{\mathbb R})\) for a complex line. Conjugation reverses its real orientation, so \(c_1(\bar L)=-c_1(L)\); the metric dual map above gives \(c_1(L^*)=-c_1(L)\).

For lines \(L,M\) on a compact Hausdorff base, choose finite embeddings \(L\hookrightarrow B\times\mathbb C^N\), \(M\hookrightarrow B\times\mathbb C^K\). Their image projections give continuous maps \(g:B\to\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\), \(h:B\to\mathbb{CP}^{K-1}\). The embedding itself identifies \(L\) with \(g^*Q_N\) and \(M\) with \(h^*Q_K\), complex linearly on every fibre. Adding a trivial coordinate if necessary makes \(N,K\ge2\).

The product chain equivalence in Paper~I, Lemma~F.4 and the free even homology in \cref{courseIII:projective-splitting-for-lesson11:section-2} give \(H_1(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\times\mathbb{CP}^{K-1};\mathbb Z)=0\) and \(H_2=\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z\), with generators the projective lines in the two axes. The universal coefficient sequence therefore shows that a degree-two integral class is determined by restriction to these two axes. Let \(Q_1,Q_2\) be the two pulled-back tautological lines. On the first axis \(Q_2\) is a fixed one-dimensional vector space, so \(Q_1\otimes Q_2\) is isomorphic to \(Q_1\); on the second it is isomorphic to \(Q_2\). These isomorphisms preserve complex orientations. Euler naturality and this degree-two detection prove

\[
c_1(Q_1\otimes Q_2)=c_1(Q_1)+c_1(Q_2).
\]

Pull back by \((g,h)\) and retain the actual bundle identifications. We obtain

\begin{equation}
c_1(L\otimes M)=c_1(L)+c_1(M),\qquad
c_1(\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb C}(L,M))=c_1(M)-c_1(L).
\label{courseIII:projective-splitting-for-lesson11:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

This applies on every compact flag space constructed above. The \cref{courseIII:integral-chern-classes-for-lesson11:section} now uses those exact maps.
