{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-FRAMED6-7", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:framed6:7", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant.md", "source_line": 62937, "source_section": "The original six-dimensional framing obstruction", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:framed6:7}

We prove that every original integral middle-homology class of a simply connected six-manifold can be represented by an embedded three-sphere whenever it is represented by a map of a three-sphere. For the two-connected \(M\) in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-6} every class has such a map by the proved Hurewicz comparison. This assertion does not require assuming that an arbitrary immersion has an even double-point count.

First retain the explicit Whitney immersion

\begin{equation}
W:S^3\subset\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R^3\longrightarrow
           \mathbb R^3\oplus\mathbb R^3,\qquad
                 W(x_0,x)=(x,x_0x).
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Its derivative on a tangent vector \((v_0,v)\), where \(x_0v_0+x\cdot v=0\), is

\begin{equation}
dW(v_0,v)=(v,v_0x+x_0v).
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

If this is zero then \(v=0\). If \(x\ne0\) the second component forces \(v_0=0\); if \(x=0\) then \(x_0=\pm1\) and the tangent constraint forces \(v_0=0\). Thus \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.1} is an immersion everywhere. Equal first components give the same \(x\). The sphere equation then allows only equal \(x_0\), or opposite \(x_0\). In the latter case, equality of the second components forces \(x_0x=0\). Distinct points therefore occur exactly at

\begin{equation}
(1,0),\quad(-1,0).
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.3}
\end{equation}

In the two local coordinate charts using \(x\), the two image tangent maps are \(v\mapsto(v,v)\) and \(v\mapsto(v,-v)\). The full concatenated determinant is

\begin{equation}
\det\begin{pmatrix}I_3&I_3\\ I_3&-I_3\end{pmatrix}
                            =\det(-2I_3)=-8\ne0.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

These two \(x\)-charts have opposite induced orientations on the boundary sphere. If both source charts are required positive, reverse the first coordinate in the south chart, and the ordered intersection determinant becomes \(+8\). Both values and their coordinate conventions are specified here; the unordered double count is one.

Translate and positively scale \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.1} into any chosen framed coordinate ball. Its homotopy class there is zero. Equations \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.21}, \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.28}, and \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:3.5} consequently give

\begin{equation}
Q_\tau(W)=0,\qquad \mu(W)=1,\qquad
                                      \varepsilon_\tau(W)=1.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.5}
\end{equation}

This proves its framing parity through the already established homotopy comparison, independently of any classification of regular homotopy classes.

Now perturb the chosen map representing a given class to a self-transverse immersion \(f\). If it has an odd number of double points, take its connected sum with a small copy of \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.1} in a coordinate ball disjoint from its image, using a thin joining arc as in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-6.1}. The ball exists because a finite union of immersed three-dimensional chart images has empty interior in a six-manifold. This operation leaves the homotopy class unchanged and changes the integer double count by exactly one, by \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:6.2}, with no intersection between the original image and that local copy. It also changes \(\varepsilon\) by one by \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:6.5} and \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.5}, so it preserves the already defined \(Q_\tau\). The resulting immersion has an even number of double points.

Pair those double points. For each pair, label its two local sheets so that the two ordered intersection signs are opposite. This is possible because interchanging two three-dimensional sheets multiplies the sign by \((-1)^9=-1\). Join the corresponding two pairs of domain preimages by disjoint arcs in \(S^3\), avoiding every other double-point preimage. Their image interiors are disjoint embedded arcs: away from the finite preimage set the immersion is one-to-one, and the two arcs can be chosen disjoint by general position in the three-dimensional domain. These arcs form a Whitney circle in \(M\).

The circle is nullhomotopic since \(M\) is simply connected. Construct its corner collars in the two original sheet charts and fill its inner curve by a disc. Relative perturbation makes this disc embedded and makes its interior disjoint from the entire immersed image and from its fixed collar. The precise dimension inequalities are

\begin{equation}
2+3<6,\qquad 2+2<6,\qquad
                 \operatorname{codim}(\text{rank }1
                          \text{ in six-by-two matrices})=5>2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.6}
\end{equation}

To apply the perturbation to the full immersed image, use its finitely many domain charts, including both charts at each double point; the same value parameters give transversality to each of these sheet images. Negative inverse-image dimension gives avoidance of every sheet. The original prescribed collar is untouched.

The rank-four adapted-frame construction and the supported Whitney move are proved in \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-5}, for the dimension pair \((3,3)\) in a six-dimensional level. That proof is local to the two chosen sheets, their arcs and the disjoint disc interior. It does not require the sheets to lie on different connected components. Apply its full frame correction, its actual sheet-coordinate comparison and its supported move here. The move removes exactly the chosen two opposite-sign double points and creates none, since its support meets the image only in those prescribed sheets. It is a regular homotopy on the domain patches; its effect on all remaining domain points is fixed.

Iteration removes all double points, since their finite number drops by two at each step. The resulting immersion is injective; a smooth injective immersion from compact \(S^3\) into a Hausdorff manifold is an embedding. It represents the original homotopy class, as every preceding operation was a homotopy of maps. This proves the claimed embedded-representative theorem with the odd-count case included.

For the two-connected framed \(M\), every integral lift \(x\) of \(a\in H_3(M;\mathbb F_2)\) therefore has an embedded representative. For every such representative the original handle-extension criterion is exactly

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 q_\tau(a)=0
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad&
 \text{some actual oriented normal framing of that embedded sphere}\\[-2pt]
 &\text{extends the specified stable frame across its }D^4\times D^3
   \text{ surgery handle.}
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.7}
\end{equation}

The proof is the equality in the second line of \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:6.22} followed by both directions of the extension construction in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-2}. No representative-dependent assumption remains. Formula \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:7.7} does not yet say that several chosen spheres can be made disjoint or that a resulting bordism has a prescribed homology; those are further geometric calculations.
