{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-FRAMED6-9", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:framed6:9", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant.md", "source_line": 63201, "source_section": "The original six-dimensional framing obstruction", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:framed6:9}

The included Pontryagin--Thom companion proves the actual map and both inverse bordism/homotopy constructions

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{PT}:\Omega^{\mathrm{fr}}_6
                                       \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }\pi_6(\mathbb S),
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.1}
\end{equation}

with the original normal and tangent frame comparison and the right-appended suspension coordinates. For arbitrary stable tangent frames in this six-dimensional bordism group, the \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-general-stable-framing} supplies the full original-involution homotopy without a complex-structure hypothesis, independently of the later sphere-recognition conclusion. Its (7.9)--(7.19) prove both inverse maps and the complete boundary-frame correction used here. The included integral stable comparison proves, with the original unit map to the two-completion and all other primary components retained, that

\begin{equation}
\pi_6(\mathbb S)\cong\mathbb Z/2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.2}
\end{equation}

The product with the actual Lie framing \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:3.6} has \(\kappa=1\) by \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:6.24} and \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13}. Thus \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13} is a nonzero homomorphism from the two-element group in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.2} to \(\mathbb F_2\). It is consequently an isomorphism: its two elements have distinct images. In particular

\begin{equation}
[S^3\times S^3,\tau_{\mathrm{Lie}}]
       \quad\hbox{is the unique nonzero element of }
                                  \Omega^{\mathrm{fr}}_6.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.3}
\end{equation}

This conclusion now uses the proved bordism invariance; the earlier product example by itself did not establish it.

Let \(\Sigma\) be the original smooth homotopy six-sphere in lesson 7, with the actual stable tangent framing constructed in the spin-six companion. Its original integral homology gives \(H_3(\Sigma;\mathbb F_2)=0\). Hence its quadratic space is the zero space, its Gauss sum is \(1\), and its Arf invariant is zero by \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:4.8}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:4.9}. Since \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13} is an isomorphism,

\begin{equation}
[\Sigma,\tau_\Sigma]=0\quad\hbox{in }\Omega^{\mathrm{fr}}_6,
 \qquad
              \operatorname{PT}(\Sigma,\tau_\Sigma)=0
                                      \quad\hbox{in }\pi_6(\mathbb S).
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.4}
\end{equation}

By the definition of framed bordism, or by the explicit inverse nullhomotopy construction in the Pontryagin--Thom companion, there is a compact smooth seven-manifold \(W\) with boundary the original \(\Sigma\) and a stable framing which restricts to the specified \(\tau_\Sigma\), after the stated appended coordinates. The boundary identification is part of this construction. Formula \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.4} has therefore supplied the actual existence of a framed nullbordism.

The conclusion is about that framed bordism and its retained boundary map. Its relative homology in degrees three and four has not been proved zero. The \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map} now constructs the new interior gluing with its full frame defect, cancels every actual handle and gives the orientation-preserving diffeomorphism to the original six-sphere, homotopic to its original degree-one map. A framed nullbordism alone does not replace that calculation.

More generally, for every closed two-connected framed six-manifold \(M\), the same proved isomorphisms give

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 M\text{ has a framed nullbordism}
   &\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \operatorname{Arf}(q_M)=0\\
   &\ \Longleftrightarrow\
       H_3(M;\mathbb F_2)\text{ has a }q_M\text{-zero Lagrangian}.
 \end{split}
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.5}
\end{equation}

The first equivalence is \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13} together with \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.3}; the second is the full constructive algebraic argument \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:4.11}. Two such framed manifolds represent the same framed bordism class exactly when their Arf values agree, because \(\kappa\) is injective. These are framed bordism statements with the specified stable frames. They do not assert that the underlying smooth manifolds are diffeomorphic.
