{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-FRAMED6-10.1", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:framed6:10.1", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant.md", "source_line": 63265, "source_section": "The original six-dimensional framing obstruction", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:framed6:10.1}

For the original family \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.22}, take \(t=9/25\). Determine its unordered double pair, the signed six-column determinant in the original sheet order, the local derivative degree at the unique singular parameter, and the resulting change of \(Q_\tau\). Retain the cubic term and every numerical coefficient.

\textbf{Solution.} The equal-output equations in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-5.5} give the pair

\begin{equation}
\{(0,0,3/5),(0,0,-3/5)\}.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.1}
\end{equation}

In both images the third coordinate is \(9/25\), and the last coordinate is

\begin{equation}
(3/5)^3-(9/25)(3/5)=27/125-27/125=0,
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.2}
\end{equation}

with the same equality after both signs are reversed. The first, second, fourth and fifth coordinates are zero. Thus the common image is \((0,0,9/25,0,0,0)\).

At the positive sheet, the three columns are

\begin{equation}
a_1=(1,0,0,3/5,0,0)^T,\quad
 a_2=(0,1,0,0,3/5,0)^T,\quad
 a_3=(0,0,6/5,0,0,18/25)^T.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.3}
\end{equation}

The negative sheet has

\begin{equation}
b_1=(1,0,0,-3/5,0,0)^T,\quad
 b_2=(0,1,0,0,-3/5,0)^T,\quad
 b_3=(0,0,-6/5,0,0,18/25)^T.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.4}
\end{equation}

The last entries are \(3(9/25)-9/25=18/25\), using the full derivative of the cubic. Subtract \(a_1,a_2\) from \(b_1,b_2\) and expand along the first two rows. The remaining determinant is

\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{-6}{5}\right)^2
   \det\begin{pmatrix}6/5&-6/5\\18/25&18/25\end{pmatrix}
    =\frac{36}{25}\frac{216}{125}
    =\frac{7776}{3125}
    =32(3/5)^5>0.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.5}
\end{equation}

Thus the double point is transverse with the positive sign in that specified order of sheet coordinates.

The singular derivative parameter is \((u,v,w,t)=(0,0,0,0)\). Equations \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.24}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.26} retain its four-component residual column \((2w,u,v,3w^2-t)\), with determinant \(-2\) at zero. The local degree is therefore \(-1\). For the actual local lift \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.11} its stable integer is \(-1\); another oriented normal frame adds the displayed even integer \(2n\) in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.12}. Hence its contribution to the change of \(\varepsilon_\tau\) is one. The family has no double point for \(t<0\) and exactly the one in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:5.27} for \(t>0\); its change of \(\mu\) is also one. Therefore

\begin{equation}
\Delta Q_\tau=1+1=0\quad\text{in }\mathbb F_2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.6}
\end{equation}

This calculation accounts for the local end of the compact double curve and for the full framing comparison. It does not equate the two signed integer contributions: the cancellation is the stated equality in \(\mathbb F_2\).
