{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-FRAMED6-10.2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:framed6:10.2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant.md", "source_line": 63328, "source_section": "The original six-dimensional framing obstruction", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:framed6:10.2}

Take the actual integral lattice with ordered basis
\((A,B,p_1,t_1,p_2,t_2)\) and intersection matrix

\begin{equation}
J=
 \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}
 \oplus
 \begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
 \oplus
 \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.7}
\end{equation}

On its reduction modulo two prescribe \(q(A)=q(B)=1\),
\(q(p_1)=q(p_2)=0\), \(q(t_1)=1\), \(q(t_2)=0\), and the quadratic law with the reduction of this exact matrix. Calculate the quadratic polynomial, the reduction by \(L=\mathbb Zp_1\oplus\mathbb Zp_2\), both fibre counts, and the Arf value. This is a specified integral algebraic example of the comparison \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.5}; no geometric realization of these extra data is assumed.

\textbf{Solution.} Write the original vector as

\begin{equation}
z=xA+yB+u_1p_1+v_1t_1+u_2p_2+v_2t_2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.8}
\end{equation}

The exact integral belt pairings are

\begin{equation}
\lambda(p_1,z)=-v_1,\qquad
                     \lambda(p_2,z)=v_2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.9}
\end{equation}

Thus \(L^\perp\) is precisely the sublattice \(v_1=v_2=0\). Its actual quotient map and inverse are

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  L^\perp/L&\longrightarrow\mathbb ZA\oplus\mathbb ZB,
       &z+L&\longmapsto xA+yB,\\
  \mathbb ZA\oplus\mathbb ZB&\longrightarrow L^\perp/L,
       &xA+yB&\longmapsto xA+yB+L.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.10}
\end{equation}

The two original signs in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.9} have been retained, although both reduce to the same nonzero sign in the coefficient field.

Iteration of the quadratic identity gives, with all coordinates now reduced modulo two,

\begin{equation}
q(z)=x+y+xy+v_1+u_1v_1+u_2v_2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.11}
\end{equation}

In particular \(q\) vanishes on the original \(L/2L\). On its perpendicular quotient \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.10}, the polynomial is exactly \(x+y+xy\). The three ordered two-coordinate Gauss factors in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.11} are respectively \(-2,2,2\), by their four-term sums in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:4.7}. Therefore

\begin{equation}
G(q)=(-2)\cdot2\cdot2=-8
             =2^2\,G(\bar q),\qquad
       \operatorname{Arf}(q)=\operatorname{Arf}(\bar q)=1.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.12}
\end{equation}

There are \(2^6=64\) original vectors modulo two. Keeping both total and signed count gives

\begin{equation}
\#q^{-1}(0)=\frac{64-8}{2}=28,\qquad
                    \#q^{-1}(1)=\frac{64+8}{2}=36.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:10.13}
\end{equation}

The zero-valued isotropic plane \(L/2L\) is not a Lagrangian: it has dimension two while half the full dimension is three. Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:4.11} and the computed Arf value prove that no zero-valued Lagrangian exists. Thus adjoining these two zero-valued belt directions has retained the nonzero Arf invariant, exactly as the complete index-three comparison \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.7} requires.
