{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-RECEIVING-1", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-receiving:1", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions.md", "source_line": 51609, "source_section": "Whitney moves in the original receiving dimensions", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-receiving:1}

The three receiving dimension triples are

\begin{equation}
(d,p,q)=(5,2,3),\quad(6,2,4),\quad(6,3,3),
 \qquad d=p+q.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

Let \(N\) be the original connected, oriented, simply connected closed \(d\)-manifold before a finite collection of disjoint framed index-\(p\) surgeries. Retain the original full attachments

\begin{equation}
\varphi_j:S^{p-1}_{r_j}\times D^{q+1}_{s_j}\longrightarrow N,
 \qquad r_j,s_j>0.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.2}
\end{equation}

Denote the resulting boundary by \(N'\) and its belt spheres by

\begin{equation}
B_j=\{0\}\times S^q_{s_j}.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.3}
\end{equation}

Let \(A_1,\ldots,A_m\) be disjoint oriented embedded \(p\)-spheres in \(N'\), with their original framed attaching maps retained, and transverse to every \(B_j\). Choose opposite-sign intersection points \(a,b\) of a particular \(A=A_i\) and \(B=B_j\).

The original complement map is

\begin{equation}
P:N'\setminus\bigcup_j B_j
    \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }
    N\setminus\bigcup_jL_j,\qquad
 L_j=\varphi_j(S^{p-1}_{r_j}\times\{0\}).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.4}
\end{equation}

It is the simultaneous disjoint-region map of the \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:belt-complement-map}, with every original radial function, cutoff integral and seam map included. Its positive-face formula, before the retained rounding comparisons, is

\begin{equation}
(u,v)\longmapsto
 \varphi_j\left(\frac{r_j}{\|u\|}u,
               \frac{F_j(\|u\|)}{s_j}v\right),
 \quad u\ne0,\quad\|v\|=s_j.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.5}
\end{equation}

All components of its derivative are given in equation \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.13a}.

The complement in \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.4} is simply connected. Indeed loops and their based null-homotopies in \(N\) can be perturbed relative their prescribed collars off the finitely many \(L_j\). The exact inequalities are

\begin{equation}
1+(p-1)<d,\qquad 2+(p-1)<d
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.6}
\end{equation}

in each of \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.1}. To see why they suffice, use finite chart perturbations whose value parameters span the normal space to \(L_j\) at each possible intersection. For a transverse parameter the inverse-image dimensions are the left sides of \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.6} minus \(d\), both negative. Sard's theorem gives such a parameter, fixed near the collars that already avoid the cores. Relative smoothing gives the required initial smooth representatives. This is exactly the finite-parameter construction already proved in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-3} of the belt-complement chapter, now with the displayed dimensions. The original inclusion induces an isomorphism on fundamental groups; \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.4} carries this assertion to the belt complement.

Construct embedded arcs \(\alpha,\beta\) from \(a\) to \(b\), respectively in \(A,B\), with interiors missing all intersection points. Both spheres have dimension at least two. Coordinate paths, finite detours around the forbidden points, removal of loops and smoothing relative endpoint germs give these arcs, as in the earlier disk proof, \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.1}. They are disjoint from one another except at the two endpoints, since their interior crossings would be forbidden intersections of \(A\) and \(B\).

At an endpoint \(c\in\{a,b\}\), use actual parametrizations

\[
 a_c(x,u)\in A,\quad u\in\mathbb R^{p-1},
 \qquad b_c(y,v)\in B,\quad v\in\mathbb R^{q-1}.
\]

Their first axes give the chosen arcs, directed away from that endpoint, with the specified original parameters. In a target chart \(\psi\), define

\begin{equation}
\mathcal H_c(x,u,y,v)=
 \psi^{-1}\bigl(\psi(a_c(x,u))+\psi(b_c(y,v))-\psi(c)\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.7}
\end{equation}

Its derivative at zero is the direct sum of the two transverse sheet tangent maps, hence invertible. It gives a full chart on a smaller original neighbourhood and satisfies the exact identities

\begin{equation}
\mathcal H_c(x,u,0,0)=a_c(x,u),\qquad
 \mathcal H_c(0,0,y,v)=b_c(y,v).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.8}
\end{equation}

The prescribed disk quadrant is \(\mathcal H_c(x,0,y,0)\), \(x,y\geq0\). Its interior avoids both sheets. The endpoint neighbourhoods can be made disjoint and avoid every other sphere.

Along \(\alpha\), extend the endpoint \(y\)-direction to a transverse vector field; its normal quotient has rank \(q\geq3\). Along \(\beta\), extend the endpoint \(x\)-direction; its normal quotient has rank \(p\geq2\). The spaces of nonzero vectors in these ranks are connected. Trivialize each quotient over the interval by the already proved projector transport, join the prescribed endpoint vectors through nonzero vectors with their end germs fixed, and interpolate the tangent components separately. Extend the fields to neighbourhoods, retaining the exact endpoint coordinate fields. Their small positive-time flows give the two strips, agreeing exactly with the endpoint quadrants. Their derivatives have rank two at time zero. The compactness and local-inverse argument in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.2} gives a uniform width on which the strips and quadrants form an embedded corner collar. Their inward sides avoid every sphere: the local defining functions have leading term \(t\nu(s)\), with \(\nu(s)\ne0\), and a uniform \(O(t^2)\) remainder; the other sheets have positive compact separation away from the endpoint charts.

Keep the same complete rounding curve with its positive width \(\epsilon\), original smooth step \(\chi\), and \(\kappa(t)=\chi(3t-1)\):

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 x(t)&=\frac{\epsilon}{2}+2\epsilon\int_0^t\kappa(s)\,ds,\\
 y(t)&=\frac{\epsilon}{2}+2\epsilon\int_t^1\kappa(1-s)\,ds,\\
 (x',y')&=2\epsilon(\kappa(t),-\kappa(1-t)),\\
 \kappa(t)+\kappa(1-t)&=1,\qquad
 \int_0^1\kappa(t)\,dt=\frac12 .
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.9}
\end{equation}

Thus its endpoints are \((\epsilon/2,3\epsilon/2)\) and \((3\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2)\); its exact vertical and horizontal germs join the strip edges at width \(\epsilon/2\). This gives the original compact corner annulus \(C_0\), with smooth inner boundary \(\gamma\), and preserves both outer corner charts.

The inner curve is in the simply connected belt complement \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.4}, so bounds a continuous disk there. Prescribe its entire inward filling collar by extending \(C_0\) smoothly a little across \(\gamma\), away from its existing interior, using the actual transverse collar field. Parametrize that filling collar by a round disk \(D_h^2\), retaining \(h>0\). The relative embedded-disk proof from \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-5} of the belt-complement chapter applies with the following explicit dimension checks. For immersions of a two-dimensional disk into a \(d\)-manifold, the rank-one stratum of two-column derivative matrices has codimension \(d-1>2\), and the rank-zero stratum has codimension \(2d>2\). The far-pair coincidence domain has dimension four, less than \(d\). For avoidance of the additional sheets and the retained annulus,

\begin{equation}
2+p<d,\qquad 2+2<d .
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.10}
\end{equation}

Their boundary and corner strata have no larger dimensions. Value-and-first-derivative chart perturbations give transversality to the rank strata; independent value perturbations at two separated points give transversality to the diagonal. Compact near-diagonal injectivity of the resulting immersion remains under a sufficiently small \(C^1\) perturbation. Sard then eliminates every listed locus. Keep the full prescribed collar fixed, and take the perturbation small enough to remain in the open belt complement. These are all the estimates used by the earlier relative construction, and they hold for every triple \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:1.1}.

The resulting embedded filling meets \(C_0\) only along its prescribed boundary and agrees with its whole continued collar there. Glue them to obtain a disk \(D\) with the two original outer corners. Its interior avoids every \(A_\ell,B_j\). The domain has the original quadrant charts, strip charts and round filling chart; their common collar agrees with all derivatives. In particular no assertion about the smooth extension of an arbitrary higher-dimensional sphere diffeomorphism has entered the disk construction.
