{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-RECEIVING-2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-receiving:2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions.md", "source_line": 51725, "source_section": "Whitney moves in the original receiving dimensions", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-receiving:2}

Both arc parameters run from \(a\) to \(b\). Write \(a'=\alpha'\), \(b'=\beta'\), and choose full tangent frames \(U=(u_1,\ldots,u_{p-1})\), \(V=(v_1,\ldots,v_{q-1})\) along the respective arcs so that \((a',U)\), \((b',V)\) have the original sheet orientations. Their endpoint germs are the original coordinate frames with these signs; interval frame transport extends them. Retain

\begin{equation}
\sigma_a=\operatorname{sgn}_{TN'}(a'_a,U_a,b'_a,V_a),\qquad
 \sigma_b=\operatorname{sgn}_{TN'}(a'_b,U_b,b'_b,V_b)
          =-\sigma_a.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

Orient \(D\) so its boundary follows \(\alpha\) forward and \(\beta\) backward. At \(a\) its tangent orientation is \((a'_a,b'_a)\). At \(b\) its quadrant coordinates point backwards along both arcs, and its disk orientation is the negative of their coordinate orientation; equivalently it is the negative of \((a'_b,b'_b)\). Moving \(b'\) past \(p-1\) columns gives

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \operatorname{sgn}_{TN'}(TD_a,U_a,V_a)
       &=(-1)^{p-1}\sigma_a,\\
 \operatorname{sgn}_{TN'}(TD_b,U_b,V_b)
       &=-(-1)^{p-1}\sigma_b
        =(-1)^{p-1}\sigma_a .
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.2}
\end{equation}

This common factor is retained.

For any original metric matrix \(G\), let \(V_0\) be the two-column disk tangent matrix and \(W=(U,V)\) the \((d-2)\)-column extra-sheet matrix. The exact normal projections and Gram matrix are

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 W^\perp&=W-V_0(V_0^{\mathsf T}GV_0)^{-1}V_0^{\mathsf T}GW,\\
 (W^\perp)^{\mathsf T}GW^\perp
 &=W^{\mathsf T}GW
   -W^{\mathsf T}GV_0(V_0^{\mathsf T}GV_0)^{-1}V_0^{\mathsf T}GW .
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.3}
\end{equation}

The Gram matrix is positive definite: a zero value would put a nonzero linear combination of \(W\) into the span of \(V_0\), contradicting the invertibility of the corner chart. The column operation taking \((V_0,W)\) to \((V_0,W^\perp)\) has determinant one, so preserves both signs \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.2}. These projections give an exact frame on each whole endpoint quadrant.

Along the \(A\)-arc, its \(p-1\) projected extra tangent vectors are independent because the disk tangent plane meets \(TA\) exactly in the arc line. Positive completions by \(q-1\) vectors form a connected space: in a quotient frame the complementary block is in \(GL^+(q-1,\mathbb R)\), and its components along the first block are arbitrary. The group \(GL^+(r,\mathbb R)\) is connected by its exact positive triangular factor and a succession of rotations for its orthogonal factor. Hence completions can be chosen smoothly along the interval, retaining both entire corner germs. Along the \(B\)-arc apply the same argument with its fixed \(q-1\) projected tangent vectors and the remaining \(p-1\) vectors. The determinant factor \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.2} puts both endpoint choices in the same positive component.

To extend these frames through the strips while preserving the exact corner frames \(e_C\), start with the normal-bundle projection connection \(P_\perp\,d\). On the corner patches change it to

\begin{equation}
\nabla^C s
 =P_\perp\,ds-
   (P_\perp\,de_C)(e_C^{\mathsf T}e_C)^{-1}e_C^{\mathsf T}s .
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.4}
\end{equation}

This displayed expression uses an auxiliary Euclidean realization of the bundle, as in the original projector construction; the original metric normal projections remain \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:2.3}. Direct substitution gives \(\nabla^C(e_C\xi)=e_C\,d\xi\). Cut off this connection correction to the disjoint corner patches, equal to it on the whole joining germs. Transport the boundary frames along each strip width. Linear ODE uniqueness makes the transported frames agree with \(e_C\) on the overlaps, and invertible transport preserves their ranks. This yields a full frame on \(C_0\), adapted to the \(A\) block on one outer arc and to the \(B\) block on the other. Extend it a little over the filling collar in the same way.

At \(d=5\) the rank-three extension and its controlled sign change are proved in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6.6}. At \(d=6\) the rank is four. We now prove that extension with every original frame coefficient retained.
