{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-RECEIVING-3", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-receiving:3", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions.md", "source_line": 51783, "source_section": "Whitney moves in the original receiving dimensions", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-receiving:3}

Use the real quaternions \(\mathbb H\) with their original ordered basis \((1,i,j,k)\), multiplication \(ij=k,jk=i,ki=j\), reversed products with minus signs, and \(i^2=j^2=k^2=-1\). Their associative matrix realization, conjugation and full norm identity are proved in \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section}, \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-2}. No alteration of the receiving disk coordinates is made by this auxiliary algebra.

For unit quaternions define

\begin{equation}
\rho:S^3\times S^3\longrightarrow SO(4),\qquad
 \rho(a,b)(x)=a x b^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Norm multiplicativity proves orthogonality. Its determinant is \(+1\), since the domain is connected and the identity pair acts as the identity. It is a homomorphism: the two left multipliers multiply in order, and the right multipliers do too after taking the inverse. If it acts trivially, substitution \(x=1\) gives \(a=b\), and then \(a\) commutes with \(i,j,k\). Comparing its four coefficients gives zero imaginary part. Thus

\begin{equation}
\ker\rho=\{(1,1),(-1,-1)\}.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

The derivative at the identity sends imaginary \(u,v\) to the skew map \(x\mapsto ux-xv\). A zero such map gives \(u=v\), which commutes with all quaternions and is imaginary, hence \(u=v=0\). Both tangent spaces have dimension six, so this derivative is an isomorphism. Translation gives local inverses everywhere. The image is open and, by compactness of \(S^3\times S^3\), closed. The group \(SO(4)\) is connected: send a first unit column to the first standard vector by a rotation in their two-plane, continue on its orthogonal complement, and end with the orientation-preserving one-dimensional identity; varying each rotation angle to zero gives a path. Therefore \(\rho\) is onto.

This is a two-sheet covering. For an image point take disjoint inverse-function neighbourhoods of its two preimages \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.2}. A smaller image neighbourhood has no other inverse images: otherwise compactness gives a limiting third preimage, contrary to \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.2}. Intersect the two inverse images with that smaller neighbourhood. These are its two disjoint local sheets. This proves the covering assertion without a classification of compact Lie groups.

Each \(S^3\) is simply connected. Smooth a loop through the original Euclidean embedding and radial retraction. Its smooth image has measure zero in the three-dimensional sphere, by Sard in coordinate charts, so misses a point. Stereographic projection from that point identifies its image with a loop in \(\mathbb R^3\), and affine contraction gives a disk. The initial smoothing is homotopic to the loop by the same retraction construction. Thus the product \(S^3\times S^3\) is simply connected too.

Lift a loop \(Q\) in \(SO(4)\), choosing its initial point. Its final point is either that initial point or its product by \((-1,-1)\). Write this endpoint sign as \(\epsilon(Q)\in\{1,-1\}\). Then

\begin{equation}
Q\text{ extends over a disk}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \epsilon(Q)=1.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.3}
\end{equation}

Necessity follows by lifting a disk, whose domain is simply connected; the lift closes on its boundary. For sufficiency, a closed lift has two quaternion loops, each contracting by the preceding construction. Their product filling followed by \(\rho\) fills \(Q\). Homotopies of loops preserve the sign: lift the homotopy with its starting points varying continuously; the endpoint difference has values in the discrete kernel \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.2} and hence is constant. Changing the initial lift multiplies both ends by the same central kernel element and also leaves the sign unchanged.

There is an exact full rotation with negative sign. Put

\begin{equation}
a(\theta)=\cos(\theta/2)+i\sin(\theta/2),\qquad
 b(\theta)=\cos(\theta/2)-i\sin(\theta/2),
 \quad 0\leq\theta\leq2\pi.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.4}
\end{equation}

Since \(b^{-1}=a\), multiplication gives

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \rho(a,b)(1)&=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta,\\
 \rho(a,b)(i)&=-\sin\theta+i\cos\theta,\\
 \rho(a,b)(j)&=j,\qquad \rho(a,b)(k)=k .
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.5}
\end{equation}

For example \(a j=j\overline a\) proves \(a j a=j\); the same identity holds for \(k\). This is one full positive rotation in the ordered \((1,i)\)-plane, fixing its orthogonal complement. Its lift ends at \((-1,-1)\). Conjugation by an oriented orthogonal comparison carries it to any specified oriented coordinate two-plane and preserves this central endpoint. Its inverse has the same negative sign.

Now let \(e\) be the original smooth oriented frame of the rank-four disk normal bundle prescribed on a whole filling collar. The projector transport used in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6.1} supplies an auxiliary oriented orthonormal frame \(n\) on the complete round filling disk \(D_h^2\). That proof uses the projection identity \(P_\perp^2=P_\perp\) and a linear ODE along radial segments; it is valid in every rank, with four initial independent vectors here. Express the prescribed full frame as

\begin{equation}
e=nR,\qquad R:D_h^2\text{ collar}\longrightarrow GL^+(4,\mathbb R).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.6}
\end{equation}

Here \(R\) is the exact matrix of all original coefficients.

Apply Gram-Schmidt with each positive length retained. For its columns \(r_j\), define

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 w_j&=r_j-\sum_{a<j}\langle q_a,r_j\rangle q_a,\\
 t_{jj}&=\|w_j\|>0,\qquad q_j=w_j/t_{jj},\\
 t_{aj}&=\langle q_a,r_j\rangle\ (a<j),\qquad t_{aj}=0\ (a>j).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.7}
\end{equation}

Every \(w_j\) is nonzero by independence of the original columns. Thus

\begin{equation}
R=QT,\qquad Q\in SO(4),\quad
 T\text{ upper triangular with }t_{jj}>0.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.8}
\end{equation}

This is an exact factorization; no entry of \(T\) is discarded. All factors depend smoothly on the collar coordinates.

The original frame extends, retaining its entire prescribed collar, precisely when the loop \(Q|_{\partial D_h^2}\) has sign \(+1\). If a full \(R\) extends, \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.7} applied to it extends \(Q\), proving necessity. For sufficiency, lift the prescribed whole collar map \(Q\) through \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.1}. A closed boundary lift makes its annular lift single-valued: extend radially by path lifting, and use the same endpoint sign on every radial circle. Choose an inner collar circle. Its two lifted quaternion loops bound disks by the argument for \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.3}. Put a product collar on these fillings and join it to the given collar using its actual radial parameter dependence; the smooth relative approximation and radial retraction, fixed on a smaller whole prescribed collar, give a smooth \(S^3\times S^3\)-valued disk extension. Its projection is an extension \(\widetilde Q\) equal to every value and derivative of the original \(Q\) on that smaller collar.

Extend the triangular factor just as explicitly. First choose \(r_0<h\) within the given collar. A smooth radial map \(r\mapsto\tau(r)\), equal to \(r\) near the boundary and constant \(r_0\) farther inside that collar, keeps its argument within the given domain. Composing each prescribed entry with \((\theta,\tau(r))\) gives an extension that is constant in the inward radial direction before leaving the collar. Choose a further radial cutoff \(\lambda\), one there and zero on a neighbourhood of the disk centre. For \(a<j\) set

\begin{equation}
\widetilde t_{aj}(r,\theta)=\lambda(r)t_{aj}(\tau(r),\theta),
 \qquad
 \widetilde t_{jj}(r,\theta)
   =\exp\bigl(\lambda(r)\log t_{jj}(\tau(r),\theta)\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.9}
\end{equation}

Where \(\tau\) is no longer needed, retain its constant value \(r_0\). The expressions are constant \(0\) off the diagonal and \(1\) on it near the centre, hence smooth there despite polar coordinates. All diagonal entries stay positive, and all original entries and their derivatives remain on the prescribed outer collar. Then

\begin{equation}
\widetilde e=n\,\widetilde Q\,\widetilde T
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.10}
\end{equation}

is the required full extending frame. It retains every original Gram and triangular factor on the original collar.

Finally, the sign can be changed while preserving the adapted outer boundary conditions of \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-2}. On a closed subarc of the \(A\)-arc, away from both corners, keep its first \(p-1\) frame vectors fixed and rotate two of the last \(q-1\) vectors by an angle from \(0\) to \(2\pi\), constant near the subarc endpoints. Such two vectors exist since \(q\geq3\). Use the same angle along the corresponding whole strip width, so the rotation is identity on the strip's end germs and extends by identity to the rest of \(C_0\). It preserves the \(A\) tangent block exactly and does not change the \(B\)-arc at all.

To check its sign for a nonorthogonal original frame, keep the comparison homotopy

\begin{equation}
R_s=Q\bigl((1-s)T+sI\bigr),\qquad 0\leq s\leq1.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.11}
\end{equation}

Every upper triangular diagonal entry is positive, so this is a homotopy through invertible matrices retaining the endpoints as stated. The loop sign is constant under it. Apply \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.11} also to \(R_s T_{\rm rot}\). At \(s=1\) the two orthogonal factors have lifts whose endpoints multiply, and \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.4}-\eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.5} give the negative factor. Therefore

\begin{equation}
\epsilon(RT_{\rm rot})=-\epsilon(R).
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.12}
\end{equation}

This is an exact comparison for the original full \(R\), not a replacement of it by \(Q\).

If the initial filling sign is negative, make this supported rotation once. Its restriction to the inner circle traverses one full rotation; reversing the circle direction has the same negative endpoint sign. The new sign is positive. Apply \eqref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.10} and glue to the unchanged corner annulus using the complete common collar. This constructs the adapted full frame over the actual disk with both original corners, in both new \(d=6\) cases. Its adjustment changes only an auxiliary disk frame. The original handle framings are transported later by the actual ambient diffeomorphism.
