{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-HANDLE-ARRANGEMENT-5.2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:handle-arrangement:5.2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/rearranging-the-original-framed-handles.md", "source_line": 48409, "source_section": "Rearranging the original framed handles", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:handle-arrangement:5.2}

In dimension six, take a finite original handle sequence with indices

\begin{equation}
(4,0,3,1,2,6,5).
\label{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:5.7}
\end{equation}

Keep the handles individually labelled, with their own original parameter radii and framings. Arrange the sequence by interchanging its leftmost adjacent inversion at each step. Compute the inversion count at every step and write the exact final map to the original cobordism in terms of the seven resulting diffeomorphisms.

\textbf{Solution.} The complete successive sequences are

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{index sequence}&I\\ \hline
(4,0,3,1,2,6,5)&7\\
(0,4,3,1,2,6,5)&6\\
(0,3,4,1,2,6,5)&5\\
(0,3,1,4,2,6,5)&4\\
(0,1,3,4,2,6,5)&3\\
(0,1,3,2,4,6,5)&2\\
(0,1,2,3,4,6,5)&1\\
(0,1,2,3,4,5,6)&0 .
\end{array}
\label{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:5.8}
\end{equation}

Initially the handle of index four contributes four inversions, the one of index three contributes two, and the one of index six contributes one; the remaining labels contribute none. Each displayed adjacent interchange removes exactly its one mutual inversion, as proved in \cref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:section-4.4}.

The first interchange uses the empty attaching face of the zero-handle. The last uses the filled component and empty belt of the top-index handle. Each of the other five uses \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:4.1}--\eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:4.13}, with its actual attaching and belt dimensions and the strict difference \(\lambda-\mu-1<0\). For example, the interchange of indices four and three has difference \(-2\); the one of four and one has difference \(-4\). These inequalities concern the cores first. The subsequent complete-tube compression and outward motion are still required, even after the cores are disjoint.

Let \(P_i\) be the presentation after step \(i\), and let
\(\Psi_i:P_{i-1}\to P_i\) be its entire diffeomorphism, including the transported later handles. If \(G_0:P_0\to C\) is the original map, the exact ordered map is

\begin{equation}
G_7
=G_0\circ\Psi_1^{-1}\circ\Psi_2^{-1}
       \circ\Psi_3^{-1}\circ\Psi_4^{-1}
       \circ\Psi_5^{-1}\circ\Psi_6^{-1}
       \circ\Psi_7^{-1}.
\label{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:5.9}
\end{equation}

For a point \(p_7\in P_7\), define successively \(p_{i-1}=\Psi_i^{-1}(p_i)\). Its complete derivative is

\begin{equation}
DG_7|_{p_7}
=DG_0|_{p_0}\,
 D\Psi_1^{-1}|_{p_1}\,
 D\Psi_2^{-1}|_{p_2}\,
 D\Psi_3^{-1}|_{p_3}\,
 D\Psi_4^{-1}|_{p_4}\,
 D\Psi_5^{-1}|_{p_5}\,
 D\Psi_6^{-1}|_{p_6}\,
 D\Psi_7^{-1}|_{p_7}.
\label{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:5.10}
\end{equation}

No two factors have been commuted. Restricting \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:5.9} to the actual outgoing boundary gives its retained final boundary identification; each factor fixes the incoming collar. All seven original handles remain, with their radii unchanged as parameter domains and their full framings carried by these derivatives. Index order alone neither deletes a handle nor proves that a final boundary diffeomorphism extends across a disk.

Illustration description: The exact original corner radii, two supported outward-flow trajectories, and the common glued pieces that interchange two handles.

\emph{Figure 1.} The left panel shows the retained radial corner curve of \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:1.5}--\eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:1.6}, with \(r=2,s=3,\delta=5,\beta=7,L=2\). Its axes are the original parameter radii \(R=\|u\|\) and \(T=\|v\|\); the full map into the original cobordism is \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:1.14}, with its derivative \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:1.15}. The right panel samples the complete cutoff flow in Exercise 5.1 for initial radii one and two. Their exact final bounds are proved there; the orange curve enters the transition annulus and therefore does not remain a constant dilation. The lower diagram records the two identical glued quotients after the whole attaching tube has moved into the common exterior. It uses all the maps in \eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:4.4}--\eqref{courseIII:rearranging-the-original-framed-handles:eq:4.13}. The source script retains the original constants; the plotted curves are numerical samples of the exact definitions.
