{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-TRAJECTORY-LOWERING-5", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:trajectory-lowering:5", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering.md", "source_line": 47511, "source_section": "Morse trajectories and supported critical-value lowering", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:trajectory-lowering:5}

Take two adjacent critical values in the wrong index order, with indices \(\mu>\lambda\). If \(\lambda\geq1\), \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:section-1} and \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:section-2} construct the entire escaping unstable disk of the upper point down to a regular level \(a<c_\mu\), above every earlier critical value. If \(\lambda=0\), use the last paragraph of \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:section-4}. Choose

\begin{equation}
a<c'<c_\mu<c_\lambda.
 \label{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

The compact support neighbourhood \(U\) can be chosen disjoint from every other critical point, because the disk is compact and contains only \(p_\lambda\). Equations \eqref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:4.4}--\eqref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:4.9} lower that point to \(c'\), leave all the other values and Hessians unchanged, and introduce no critical point. The two values have exchanged order. They may coincide at one intermediate parameter; at that parameter both critical points still have nonsingular Hessians, so the function remains Morse. Distinct critical values are restored at the endpoint.

For a finite critical sequence with indices \(k_1,\ldots,k_N\), let

\begin{equation}
I=\#\{(i,j):i<j,\ k_i>k_j\}.
 \label{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

Exchanging an adjacent wrong-order pair removes its mutual inversion and changes none of its total comparisons with any third entry. Thus \(I\) decreases by exactly one. Repeating the proved construction terminates after finitely many steps, with an actual Morse function \(f_{\rm ord}\) and an actual descending field \(Z_{\rm ord}\) on \(C\), whose critical values occur in nondecreasing index order. Every step keeps the original boundary collars. No handle or critical point has yet been removed.

The original Morse construction now applies to \((f_{\rm ord},Z_{\rm ord})\) itself. Keep the original handle parameter radii \(r_i,s_i\) for each labelled critical point. Choose the new positive critical-band widths explicitly within the new critical-value gaps, and label them \(\delta_i^{\rm ord},\beta_i^{\rm ord}\); they are additional retained choices, not assertions that the old widths fit new gaps. Every original Hessian coefficient is unchanged. The resulting handle chart has the exact formula

\begin{equation}
x_{i,j}
 =\frac{u_j}{r_i}
   \sqrt{\frac{\delta_i^{\rm ord}
          +\beta_i^{\rm ord}\|v\|^2/s_i^2}{a_{i,j}}},
 \qquad
 y_{i,l}=\frac{v_l}{s_i}
              \sqrt{\frac{\beta_i^{\rm ord}}{b_{i,l}}}.
 \label{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:5.3}
\end{equation}

The original chapter supplies its full tangent map, seam, rounding and final map into \(C\), using these retained quantities. Let that complete map be
\(G_{\rm ord}^{\,M}:P_{\rm ord}^{\,M}\to C\). For the previously constructed original presentation \(G_{\rm old}:P_{\rm old}\to C\), the exact comparison is

\begin{equation}
\Psi_M=(G_{\rm ord}^{\,M})^{-1}G_{\rm old},
 \qquad
 G_{\rm ord}^{\,M}=G_{\rm old}\Psi_M^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:5.4}
\end{equation}

Its derivative is the full chain

\begin{equation}
D\Psi_M|_z
 =D(G_{\rm ord}^{\,M})^{-1}|_{G_{\rm old}(z)}
       DG_{\rm old}|_z.
 \label{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:eq:5.5}
\end{equation}

In particular its restriction to the outgoing boundary is retained. This is the precise relation to the earlier handle presentation. It does not assert that changed trajectory fields leave each thick attaching embedding fixed, or that the Morse presentation is literally the same as the separately reordered presentation.

We now have the function and trajectories needed for later geometric cancellations. The \cref{courseIII:removing-the-original-zero-and-top-handles:zero-supported-cancellation} is now proved in its receiving companion: it constructs the connected trajectory graph, verifies each cancellation hypothesis and retains all reversal signs. The \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-index-one-cancellation} now use this exact holonomy and supported lowering to remove index one and its reverse. Its \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6} and \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-7} compare the complement and descending maps on their actual domains and verify every cancellation hypothesis. The \cref{courseIII:integral-handle-chains-and-signed-incidences:handle-six-seven} now proves the original filtration-to-homology map, every incidence sign, the full integer basis comparisons and the exact contractions in dimensions six and seven. The \cref{courseIII:higher-index-framed-slides-and-chain-maps:higher-slide-matrices} now realizes every elementary integral change with its full framed cobordism map and proves both neighbouring differentials using the singular-prism correction. The \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:receiving-whitney-flow} proves the full disk, adapted frame and supported move for the three required sheet-dimension pairs, with all original metric, orientation and framing maps retained. The \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:middle-six-seven-reduction} constructs the compatible function, proves the exact intersection-to-trajectory comparison and every escape and support hypothesis, and completes the simply connected smooth cobordism reductions in dimensions six and seven. \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:section-5} of that receiving companion verifies every supported-cancellation hypothesis for the resulting trajectories. The \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map} supplies the additional classification calculation. None of those later steps is asserted by the index-order proof.
