{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-BELT-COMPLEMENT-2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:belt-complement:2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk.md", "source_line": 49754, "source_section": "Belt-sphere complements and the Whitney disk", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:belt-complement:2}

We next prove what deleting the cores does to homotopy groups. This proof is for the given framed tubes, so it neither assumes a global transversality theorem nor suppresses the normal-coordinate data.

Let \(P\) be a compact smooth manifold, possibly with boundary, of dimension \(r<q\). Let \(u:P\to N\) be smooth and suppose its values on a closed prescribed set \(Q\subset P\) avoid the cores. We will find an ambient isotopy \(T_t:N\to N\) such that \(T_tu=u\) on \(Q\) for all \(t\), and \(T_1u(P)\) misses all the cores.

For one tube, the compact set \(u(Q)\) is disjoint from \(L\). Choose a radius \(0<\delta<b\) so that

\begin{equation}
u(Q)\cap\varphi(S_a^{k-1}\times D_\delta^q)=\varnothing.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

To see that such a choice exists, otherwise a sequence of points of the compact \(u(Q)\) would have normal radii tending to zero in the compact closed tube, and a subsequence would limit to a point of \(u(Q)\cap L\). This is a contradiction. If \(Q\) is empty, choose any \(\delta\) in the displayed interval.

Choose a smooth function \(\zeta:\mathbb R^q\to[0,1]\), equal to one near zero and supported in \(\|z\|<\delta\). For instance use a smooth function of \(\|z\|^2\), built from \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.7}, constant near zero. Set

\begin{equation}
L_\zeta=\sup_{z\in\mathbb R^q}\|D\zeta(z)\|.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.2}
\end{equation}

This is finite because the derivative has compact support. Choose a vector \(v\in\mathbb R^q\) with

\begin{equation}
\|v\|L_\zeta<1.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.3}
\end{equation}

For \(0\leq t\leq1\), define in the original framed tube

\begin{equation}
T_t\bigl(\varphi(\theta,z)\bigr)
=\varphi(\theta,z+t\zeta(z)v),
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.4}
\end{equation}

and define \(T_t\) to be the identity outside the tube.

We verify that these formulas give diffeomorphisms for all \(t\), rather than assume the shifted point lies in the tube. Extend the normal map to all of \(\mathbb R^q\). The mean-value inequality gives

\begin{equation}
\|(z+t\zeta(z)v)-(z'+t\zeta(z')v)\|
\geq(1-t\|v\|L_\zeta)\|z-z'\|.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.5}
\end{equation}

Thus it is injective. Given \(y\in\mathbb R^q\), the iteration

\begin{equation}
z_{\nu+1}=y-t\zeta(z_\nu)v
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.6}
\end{equation}

has consecutive differences bounded by a geometric sequence with ratio \(t\|v\|L_\zeta<1\). Their sum is finite, so the sequence is Cauchy, converges in \(\mathbb R^q\), and its limit solves \(z+t\zeta(z)v=y\). The same estimate proves uniqueness. The derivative is

\begin{equation}
I_q+t\,v\otimes D\zeta(z),\qquad
\det(I_q+t\,v\otimes D\zeta(z))
=1+t\,D\zeta(z)v>0.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.7}
\end{equation}

The determinant identity follows by multilinearity of columns: any term using two columns of the rank-one addition vanishes, leaving the identity term and the trace of that addition. The strict bound is \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.3}. Local inverse maps are smooth and agree by uniqueness, giving a global smooth inverse, smoothly depending on \(t\).

The entire normal derivative inverse, useful for retaining the framing, is

\begin{equation}
\bigl(I_q+t\,v\otimes D\zeta(z)\bigr)^{-1}
=I_q-\frac{t\,v\otimes D\zeta(z)}
{1+t\,D\zeta(z)v}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.7a}
\end{equation}

Multiplication gives the identity because
\((v\otimes D\zeta)^2=(D\zeta\,v)(v\otimes D\zeta)\).
The full denominator is positive by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.3}, so this formula has no exceptional point in its stated domain.

This normal diffeomorphism fixes every point with \(\|z\|\geq\delta\). It therefore maps \(D_b^q\) onto itself: an interior point could not map to a fixed exterior point, by injectivity, and the same argument applies to the inverse. Hence \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.4} is defined in the original tube. It is the identity near the tube boundary and on \(u(Q)\), by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.1}, so it pastes smoothly with the exterior identity. We have proved the claimed ambient isotopy for any vector satisfying \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.3}.

It remains to choose \(v\) so that the final image misses the core. On the open subset of \(P\) where \(u(p)\) lies in the interior of the tube, write the actual inverse coordinates as

\begin{equation}
\varphi^{-1}u(p)=(\theta(p),z(p)).
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.8}
\end{equation}

If \(\zeta(z(p))=0\), the isotopy leaves its nonzero normal coordinate fixed, since \(\zeta=1\) at zero. If \(\zeta(z(p))>0\), intersection with the core at time one occurs exactly when

\begin{equation}
v=-\frac{z(p)}{\zeta(z(p))}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.9}
\end{equation}

The map on the right is smooth on that open subset. Its image has \(q\)-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero because \(r<q\). Here is the precise elementary estimate. Exhaust the subset where \(\zeta(z(p))>0\) by countably many compact coordinate pieces on which this map and its first derivatives are bounded; the sets where \(\zeta(z(p))\geq1/m\), followed by finite coordinate refinements, suffice. Its restriction to each sufficiently small coordinate box is Lipschitz. Cover its bounded \(r\)-dimensional coordinate domain by \(O(h^{-r})\) cubes of side \(h\). Each image lies in a \(q\)-dimensional ball of radius at most a constant times \(h\). The total volume of these covering balls is \(O(h^{q-r})\), which tends to zero. Thus each compact piece has zero outer measure, and so does their countable union. The same argument covers boundary charts by half-cubes.

Every sufficiently small open ball of parameter vectors has positive \(q\)-dimensional volume. Choose \(v\) in that ball outside the measure-zero set \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.9}, also satisfying \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.3}. Then \(T_1u\) misses the core. For finitely many disjoint tubes, make these choices in each tube. Their isotopies have disjoint supports and preserve each tube, so their composition avoids every core and fixes \(u(Q)\). This proves the avoidance assertion with all prescribed data fixed.

To apply it to continuous homotopy representatives, we supply the smoothing step. The explicit finite-coordinate embedding and normal-neighbourhood construction in the included CW companion, Paper~I, Subsection~G.2, applies to the compact smooth \(N\); denote its embedding by \(\iota:N\to\mathbb R^M\), normal neighbourhood by \(U\), and retraction by \(R:U\to N\). Let \(u:P\to N\) be continuous and smooth near the prescribed closed set \(Q\). The compact image of \(F=\iota u\) has a positive Euclidean margin inside \(U\). Choose a finite cover of \(P\) on which the variation of \(F\) is smaller than any given positive error, a smooth subordinate partition \(\psi_i\), and points \(p_i\) in those sets. Then

\begin{equation}
F_0(p)=\sum_i\psi_i(p)F(p_i)
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.10}
\end{equation}

is smooth and uniformly within that error of \(F\). Choose a smooth cutoff \(\sigma\) equal to one near \(Q\), supported where \(F\) is smooth, and put

\begin{equation}
\widetilde F=\sigma F+(1-\sigma)F_0.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.11}
\end{equation}

It is smooth, equals \(F\) near \(Q\), and is as close to \(F\) as \(F_0\) is. The entire straight segment \((1-t)F+t\widetilde F\) lies in \(U\) when the error is below the stated compact margin. Applying \(R\) gives a homotopy from \(u\) to the smooth map \(R\widetilde F\), fixed near \(Q\).

The partitions and cutoff here are obtained from finitely many coordinate bumps of the form \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.7}, divided by their positive sum, exactly as in the included manifold embedding. If only the boundary values are initially smooth, first make the map constant in the collar direction near the boundary. On a disk, replace its radius \(r\) by a smooth function \(\kappa(r)\) equal to \(r\) away from the boundary and equal to one near the boundary, with \(r\leq\kappa(r)\leq1\). Straight interpolation of these radial functions is a homotopy fixed on the boundary. It keeps the centre fixed by using the identity near zero. The composite with the original map is now smooth in a boundary collar.

For a based homotopy on \(D^i\times I\), its endpoint representatives may first be made constant in a common collar of \(\partial D^i\). Choose the spatial radial change supported entirely in that collar; it fixes both endpoint representatives exactly. Compose the homotopy with this spatial change and with a time-coordinate change constant near each endpoint of \(I\). Near the spatial side it is now the constant basepoint, and near the two time ends it is the prescribed smooth endpoint map, constant in the time direction. These values agree in neighbourhoods of their corners. This makes the map smooth near the entire prescribed boundary without changing that boundary. Formulas \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.10}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.11} then apply relative it. If all values already lie in the open complement of the cores, choose the uniform error below its additional positive compact margin. Its smoothing homotopy then stays in that complement.
