{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-BELT-COMPLEMENT-3", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:belt-complement:3", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk.md", "source_line": 49875, "source_section": "Belt-sphere complements and the Whitney disk", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:belt-complement:3}

Assume \(q=d-k\geq2\), and choose a basepoint in the common exterior \(A\). Let \(U=N\setminus\bigcup_jL_j\). The inclusion

\begin{equation}
\iota_U:U\hookrightarrow N
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

is a bijection on components. Surjectivity follows by moving any point off the cores with the dimension-zero case of \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-2}. If two points in \(U\) are connected in \(N\), smooth a connecting path relative its two endpoints and apply the avoidance argument with \(r=1<q\). Its final path lies in \(U\) with exactly the same endpoints. This proves injectivity on components.

For each \(1\leq i\leq q-1\), a based \(\pi_i(N)\) representative is a disk map with its whole boundary at the basepoint. Smooth it relative a constant boundary collar as just proved. Its domain has dimension \(i<q\); avoidance fixes its boundary and gives a representative in \(U\) of the same class in \(N\). Thus

\begin{equation}
(\iota_U)_*:\pi_i(U)\longrightarrow\pi_i(N)
\quad\text{is surjective for }1\leq i\leq q-1.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

For \(1\leq i\leq q-2\), take two based representatives in \(U\) which become homotopic in \(N\). Smooth the representatives within \(U\), retaining their based boundary collars. Their homotopy in \(N\) can then be smoothed relative these two endpoint maps and the constant based boundary. Its domain has dimension \(i+1<q\). Apply avoidance relative that entire prescribed boundary. The resulting homotopy lies in \(U\) and has the original smoothed endpoint maps. Composing with the smoothing homotopies inside \(U\) proves injectivity. Consequently

\begin{equation}
(\iota_U)_*:\pi_i(U)\xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }\pi_i(N)
\quad(1\leq i\leq q-2),
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.3}
\end{equation}

and \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.2} retains the surjection in the next degree. These assertions preserve the original basepoint, not merely an abstract isomorphism type.

Through \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.14} the map on the outgoing belt complement is the actual composite

\begin{equation}
N'\setminus\bigcup_jB_j
\xrightarrow{\Phi}U
\xrightarrow{\iota_U}N.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.4}
\end{equation}

It is the identity on \(A\), and its maps on homotopy groups have precisely the properties \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.2}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.3}. This gives an explicit correspondence between both complements and the earlier handle level.

In the six-dimensional case relevant to lesson 7, \(d=6\), \(k=2\), and \(q=4\). Thus the original attaching spheres are circles, their normal disks have dimension four, and their outgoing belt spheres have dimension three. Formula \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.4} induces

\begin{equation}
\pi_1\!\left(N'\setminus\bigcup_jB_j\right)\cong\pi_1(N),
\qquad
\pi_2\!\left(N'\setminus\bigcup_jB_j\right)\cong\pi_2(N),
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.5}
\end{equation}

and a surjection in degree three. All these maps have been constructed above. In particular, a simply connected preceding level gives a simply connected complement of the full belt-sphere system. The full index-two framing and the original positive radii occur in \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.11}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.14}, so this conclusion is not based on a changed surgery presentation.

This supplies the complement in which a pushed-off Whitney circle can be filled. \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6} and \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7} use this exact complement to construct the boundary collar, embedded filling and required full normal frame for the specified handle stage. The preceding middle handle level must still be obtained by reducing the actual cobordism. The \cref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:move-receiving-attachments} is proved in its included companion. Equations \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.4}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.5} supply the complement map used by the complete disk construction; they do not by themselves prove the later handle reduction.

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:belt-complement-diagram}
Illustration description: The two original complements, their inverse diffeomorphisms, the exact seam identity and the resulting homotopy maps.

\emph{\cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-1} and \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-3}, equations \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.11}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.14}, \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.4}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.9}, and \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.4}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.5}. This is a diagram of the actual maps, not a lower-dimensional geometric substitute for the handle. The original attaching framing appears in both coordinate formulas, the two radial seams match exactly, and the integer homotopy comparison keeps the common exterior and its basepoint.}
