{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-BELT-COMPLEMENT-4", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:belt-complement:4", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk.md", "source_line": 49936, "source_section": "Belt-sphere complements and the Whitney disk", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:belt-complement:4}

\textbf{Exercise 4.1 --- Repair the seam without changing its radii.} Use the cutoff \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.7} with \(a=2\), \(b=3\), and \(\varepsilon=1/8\). Compute \(I_\eta,c\), the complete radial map on its three intervals, and both seam derivatives of the unrepaired and repaired maps.

\textbf{Solution.} Formula \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.8a}, including its two contributing intervals, gives

\begin{equation}
I_\eta=\frac18+\frac1{16}=\frac3{16},\qquad
c=\frac{3-3/16}{2-3/16}=\frac{45}{29}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

The transition endpoints are \(a-2\varepsilon=7/4\) and \(a-\varepsilon=15/8\), and the exact cutoff argument is \(8r-14\). Thus

\begin{equation}
f(r)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{45r}{29},&0\leq r\leq7/4,\\[2mm]
\dfrac{315}{116}+
\displaystyle\int_{7/4}^{r}
\left(\dfrac{45}{29}-\dfrac{16}{29}\chi(8s-14)\right)\,ds,
&7/4\leq r\leq15/8,\\[2mm]
r+1,&15/8\leq r\leq2.
\end{cases}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.2}
\end{equation}

The lower joining value is \(45(7/4)/29=315/116\). In the middle interval the full integral is

\begin{equation}
\frac{45}{29}\frac18-\frac{16}{29}\frac1{16}
=\frac{37}{232}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.3}
\end{equation}

Adding it to \(315/116\) gives \(667/232=23/8\), exactly the value of \(r+1\) at \(15/8\). Flatness of the cutoff at both endpoints makes all derivative joins agree as well. At the original seam \(r=2\), \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.2} gives \(f(2)=3\) and \(f'(2)=1\), with every higher derivative zero in a neighbourhood. Therefore \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.13} matches the original exterior collar exactly.

In contrast, the map \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.4} has original normal radius \(3r/2\). Its derivative at the inner seam is \(3/2\), while the exterior identity has derivative one. Its endpoint radius is correct, but that first derivative prevents it from being smooth in the specified collars. Formula \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.2} repairs precisely this defect while retaining both radii and every term of the transition integral.

\textbf{Exercise 4.2 --- Why the normal dimension in \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.3} matters.} Let \(R>0\) and

\begin{equation}
N=S_R^3=\{(z_1,z_2)\in\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=R^2\},
\qquad
L=\{(z_1,0):|z_1|=R\}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.4}
\end{equation}

Give \(L\) its displayed normal complex coordinate, so \(q=2\). Prove that \(\pi_1(N\setminus L)\to\pi_1(N)\) is surjective but not injective.

\textbf{Solution.} The framing is actual: for \(0<b<R\), use

\begin{equation}
\varphi:S_R^1\times D_b^2\longrightarrow S_R^3,\qquad
\varphi(\theta,z)=
\left(\frac{\sqrt{R^2-|z|^2}}R\,\theta,z\right).
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.5}
\end{equation}

Its two coordinates have squared norms \(R^2-|z|^2\) and \(|z|^2\), so the image lies in the original sphere. The first factor is positive on this entire tube; its inverse recovers \(z\) directly and then \(\theta=Rz_1/\sqrt{R^2-|z|^2}\). Hence it is a framed tube about \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.4}.

On the full complement \(z_2\ne0\), define

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Upsilon:S_R^3\setminus L&\longrightarrow S_R^1\times\mathbb C,\\
\Upsilon(z_1,z_2)&=
\left(\frac R{|z_2|}z_2,\frac R{|z_2|}z_1\right),\\
\Upsilon^{-1}(\lambda,w)&=
\left(\frac{Rw}{\sqrt{R^2+|w|^2}},
\frac{R\lambda}{\sqrt{R^2+|w|^2}}\right).
\end{aligned}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:4.6}
\end{equation}

All denominators are positive in their domains. The inverse's squared norm is
\(R^2(|w|^2+R^2)/(R^2+|w|^2)=R^2\), and its second coordinate is nonzero. Substitution in both directions returns the original coordinates, with \(|\lambda|=R\). These are inverse smooth maps. Contracting \(w\) linearly shows that this complement has the homotopy type of the original circle \(S_R^1\).

Its fundamental group is \(\mathbb Z\): a based loop lifts to a real angle through \(t\mapsto R e^{2\pi it}\); successive short arc charts construct its unique lift starting at zero. Its final angle is an integer. This integer is unchanged in a based homotopy, by continuity of the lifted endpoints. A lift ending at zero contracts linearly with endpoints fixed, so the endpoint integer is also a complete invariant. The loop \(t\mapsto R e^{2\pi imt}\) supplies every integer \(m\).

The two stereographic charts of \(S_R^3\) are contractible and their intersection is path connected. Van Kampen therefore gives \(\pi_1(S_R^3)=0\). Thus \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.1} induces the map \(\mathbb Z\to0\), which is surjective and is not injective. This is exactly the \(q=2\) range of \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.2}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.3}. The \(q=4\) conclusion \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.5} rests on its larger normal dimension, with the strict disk-avoidance inequality proved in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-2}.
