{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-SUPPORT-3", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-support:3", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-move-with-controlled-support.md", "source_line": 50906, "source_section": "The supported Whitney move and its original framings", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-support:3}

We first prove the coordinate extension that the original disk needs. The radius \(h>0\) in this section is the radius of its original round filling disk. A collar germ means a map defined on some whole open band about the boundary; two such maps define the same germ if they agree on a smaller whole band.

\textbf{Collar extension.} A smooth diffeomorphism germ from a collar of \(\partial D_h^2\) to itself, taking the inward side to the inward side, extends to a diffeomorphism of \(D_h^2\) that agrees with that entire germ on a smaller collar.

First suppose the boundary map preserves orientation. Write its angular lift as \(f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\), with

\begin{equation}
f(\theta+2\pi)=f(\theta)+2\pi,\qquad f'(\theta)>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Choose a smooth function \(a_0:[0,h]\to[0,1]\), zero near \(0\) and one near \(h\). The maps

\begin{equation}
F_s(r,\theta)=
\bigl(r,\ \theta+s\,a_0(r)(f(\theta)-\theta)\bigr),
\qquad 0\leq s\leq1,
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

are diffeomorphisms of the disk. They are the identity near its centre. At each positive radius their angular derivative is

\begin{equation}
1+s\,a_0(r)(f'(\theta)-1)
=(1-s\,a_0(r))+s\,a_0(r)f'(\theta)>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.3}
\end{equation}

The angular map has degree one by \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.1}, hence is bijective. Its inverse is smooth by \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.3}. The radial coordinate remains \(r\), so this proves global bijectivity and smoothness of the inverse, including at the centre.

Let \(k\) be the given collar germ. The germ \(k_0=F_1^{-1}k\) fixes the boundary pointwise. Use inward coordinates \((\theta,t)\), where \(t=h-r\). Smoothness across \(t=0\), and preservation of the inward side, give

\begin{equation}
k_0(\theta,t)=
\bigl(\theta+c(\theta)t+O(t^2),\
       a(\theta)t+O(t^2)\bigr),\qquad a(\theta)>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.4}
\end{equation}

The positive normal factor \(a(\theta)\) is part of the original germ; it is not discarded. Define

\begin{equation}
D_s(\theta,t)=\bigl(\theta,a_s(\theta)t\bigr),\qquad
a_s(\theta)=(1-s)+s\,a(\theta)>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.5}
\end{equation}

The velocity of this local isotopy at its current point is

\begin{equation}
X_s(\theta,t)=\frac{a(\theta)-1}{a_s(\theta)}
                  t\,\partial_t.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.6}
\end{equation}

Multiply \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.6} by a smooth collar cutoff, equal to one sufficiently close to the boundary and zero off a slightly larger collar. This gives a globally defined smooth time-dependent field on the disk. It vanishes on the boundary and near the centre. Its flow exists for \(0\leq s\leq1\) by compactness. It preserves the boundary and the inward side by uniqueness, and has an inverse obtained by running the time-dependent equation backwards.

For a sufficiently small original \(t\), every path \(a_s(\theta)t\) remains in the region where the cutoff is one: the maximum of \(a_s\) is finite on the compact circle and time interval. Uniqueness therefore makes the global flow agree there with the full germ \(D_s\). Write its time-one map as \(\widetilde D\).

The residual germ \(g=D_1^{-1}k_0\) fixes the boundary and has identity normal derivative there. In circle coordinates its exact form is

\begin{equation}
g(\theta,t)=
\bigl(\theta+t\,C(\theta,t),\
       t+t^2 B(\theta,t)\bigr).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.7}
\end{equation}

The integral Taylor formula gives smooth periodic coefficient functions. The angular difference uses the unique small lift near the identity. For \(\delta_s(\theta,t)=(\theta,st)\), the exact conjugate is

\begin{equation}
g_s=\delta_s^{-1}g\delta_s,\qquad
g_s(\theta,t)=
\bigl(\theta+s t\,C(\theta,st),\
       t+s t^2B(\theta,st)\bigr).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.8}
\end{equation}

The right side defines it smoothly also at \(s=0\), where it is the identity. At the boundary,

\begin{equation}
Dg_s|_{t=0}=
\begin{pmatrix}1&sC(\theta,0)\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.9}
\end{equation}

The parameter-dependent inverse function theorem and compactness give one collar on which these are local diffeomorphisms for all \(s\). They are injective on a smaller common collar: otherwise equal-image pairs in collars of shrinking width would have both limits at the same boundary point, since their boundary maps are the identity, contradicting the local inverse there.

The velocity \(\partial_sg_s\circ g_s^{-1}\) is smooth on this common collar and vanishes on its boundary. Cut it off outside a smaller collar. For inputs in a sufficiently small further collar, the whole paths \(g_s(\theta,t)\) stay in the region where the cutoff is one, uniformly by \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.8}. The resulting global flow therefore agrees with \(g_s\) there. Call its time-one map \(\widetilde g\). Then

\begin{equation}
Q=F_1\,\widetilde D\,\widetilde g
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.10}
\end{equation}

extends \(F_1D_1g=k\) on a whole collar, with all derivatives.

If the original boundary map reverses orientation, retain the reflection

\begin{equation}
J(p_1,p_2)=(p_1,-p_2),\qquad
DJ=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\quad\det DJ=-1.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.11}
\end{equation}

Apply the proved construction to \(Jk\), then compose its extension on the left by \(J\). This extends the original \(k\), retaining the reflection and its sign. Both cases are proved.
