{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-SUPPORT-6", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-support:6", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-move-with-controlled-support.md", "source_line": 51376, "source_section": "The supported Whitney move and its original framings", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-support:6}

Choose \(d,b>0\) so the compact \(K_{d,b}\) of \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.4} lies in this \(U\), and define

\begin{equation}
H_t=
\begin{cases}
\Psi\mathcal H_t\Psi^{-1},&\text{on }\Psi(U),\\
\mathrm{id},&\text{off }\Psi(U).
\end{cases}
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

Equivalently push forward \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.5} and extend it by zero. Its support is compactly contained in \(\Psi(U)\), so this gives a smooth complete field and \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.1} is its global flow. The inverse is \(H_{-t}\). At \(m=\Psi(x,y,z)\), the full derivative is

\begin{equation}
DH_t|_m=
D\Psi|_{\mathcal H_t(x,y,z)}
\,D\mathcal H_t|_{(x,y,z)}
\,D\Psi^{-1}|_m.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.2}
\end{equation}

The two derivatives of \(\Psi\) are evaluated at their different actual points. In coordinate volumes,

\begin{equation}
\det DH_t|_m=
\frac{\det D\Psi|_{\mathcal H_t(x,y,z)}}
{\det D\Psi|_{(x,y,z)}}J_t>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.3}
\end{equation}

The quotient is retained. Its sign is positive because the two nonzero determinants have the same sign on the connected coordinate neighbourhood.

Equations \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.8}--\eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.11} and \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:5.17} prove that \(H_1(A)\) has neither selected intersection with \(B\), and has exactly its original intersections outside the support. No new intersection with another core occurs: each such core is fixed pointwise, and an injective diffeomorphism fixing it cannot carry a point outside it into it. All the other attaching cores remain fixed. The selected \(B\) is the comparison set in this intersection statement; the ambient map is not required to fix its points.

At the transverse times, the original signs are \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.11} multiplied by the orientation sign of \(\Psi\) and the two signs comparing the model parameters with the original oriented sheets. These signs are constant along the connected arcs. They give precisely the original \(\sigma_p,\sigma_q\) at time zero and remain opposite. The unique tangency time remains \(R^2/(R^2+d)\).

Retain all the original framed attaching tubes at this level,

\begin{equation}
\varphi_\ell:S_{a_\ell}^{k_\ell-1}\times D_{b_\ell}^{q_\ell}
\longrightarrow N'.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.4}
\end{equation}

Transport the entire collection by

\begin{equation}
\varphi_{\ell,t}=H_t\circ\varphi_\ell,\qquad
D\varphi_{\ell,t}|_\xi
=DH_t|_{\varphi_\ell(\xi)}D\varphi_\ell|_\xi.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.5}
\end{equation}

Their original domains, radii and disjointness are retained. Equations \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:2.1}--\eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:2.4} and \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.2} supply every derivative factor. For the other attaching cores, \(H_t\) is identity on a whole neighbourhood, so their core maps and derivative framings are unchanged.

Avoiding another core does not imply avoiding every point of its original thick tube. Its outer part can meet the support. Moving only the chosen tube while keeping all those thick images unchanged would not prove disjointness. Formula \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.5} transports the full original collection by one diffeomorphism, preserving all radii and mutual disjointness while fixing the other framed cores.
