{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-SUPPORT-7.1", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-support:7.1", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-move-with-controlled-support.md", "source_line": 51483, "source_section": "The supported Whitney move and its original framings", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-support:7.1}

Take \(R=2\), \(d=3/5\), retaining any positive \(b\) small enough for the support inclusion. Find the cancellation time, both intersection positions and signs at \(t=1/2\), and the full second spatial and first time derivatives at the tangency.

\textbf{Solution.} Equations \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.9}--\eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.11} give

\begin{equation}
t_*=\frac{4}{4+3/5}=\frac{20}{23},\qquad
x_\pm(1/2)=\pm\sqrt{4-\frac35}=\pm\sqrt{\frac{17}{5}}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

The determinants are \(-2(1-1/2)x_\pm=-x_\pm\), respectively positive and negative. At \((t,x)=(20/23,0)\), the exact derivatives are

\begin{equation}
\partial_x^2\bigl((1-t)(4-x^2)-3t/5\bigr)=-\frac6{23},
\qquad
\partial_t\bigl((1-t)(4-x^2)-3t/5\bigr)=-\frac{23}{5}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

At \(t=1\) the central expression is \(-3/5\). Where the \(x\)-cutoff transitions, \(\phi\) was already negative and the displacement is nonnegative downwards. The normal cutoff is one at \(z=0\), the only possible intersection normal coordinate. Thus no transition region or omitted normal coordinate produces an extra intersection.
