{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-WHITNEY-SUPPORT-7.2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:whitney-support:7.2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/whitney-move-with-controlled-support.md", "source_line": 51509, "source_section": "The supported Whitney move and its original framings", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:whitney-support:7.2}

Let \(a(\theta)=2+\tfrac12\cos\theta\), and consider the actual collar germ

\begin{equation}
k(\theta,t)=
\bigl(\theta+t\sin\theta,\ a(\theta)t+t^2\cos\theta\bigr).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.3}
\end{equation}

Its boundary map is identity. Compute the exact residual after its positive normal factor is removed by the comparison of \cref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:section-3}, keeping the image-angle evaluation. Then compute the full normal-coordinate relation for

\begin{equation}
T(p)=
\begin{pmatrix}
2&p_1&p_2\\
0&3&p_1+p_2\\
0&0&5
\end{pmatrix}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Solution.} The inverse of \(D_1(\theta,t)=(\theta,a(\theta)t)\) divides its second coordinate by \(a\) evaluated at the input angle of that inverse. Therefore

\begin{equation}
g(\theta,t)=
\left(\theta+t\sin\theta,\
\frac{a(\theta)t+t^2\cos\theta}
     {a(\theta+t\sin\theta)}\right).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.5}
\end{equation}

The denominator is at least \(3/2\). To retain all terms, use

\begin{equation}
a(\theta+t\sin\theta)-a(\theta)
=t\sin\theta\int_0^1a'(\theta+\tau t\sin\theta)\,d\tau.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.6}
\end{equation}

The second component in \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.5} is exactly

\begin{equation}
t+t^2\,
\frac{\cos\theta-\sin\theta\int_0^1
a'(\theta+\tau t\sin\theta)\,d\tau}
{a(\theta+t\sin\theta)}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.7}
\end{equation}

This is \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.7} with the complete original angular contribution. Its dilation conjugate replaces \(t\) by \(st\) inside the coefficient of \(t^2\), multiplies that correction by \(s\), and has first component \(\theta+st\sin\theta\). It is smooth at \(s=0\).

For \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.4}, \(\det T=30>0\), and

\begin{equation}
T(p)z=(2z_1+p_1z_2+p_2z_3,\
3z_2+(p_1+p_2)z_3,\ 5z_3).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.8}
\end{equation}

The tube comparison is the full \(T_{eT}(p,z)=T_e(p,T(p)z)\). Its differential first sends

\begin{equation}
(v,w)\longmapsto
\bigl(v,\ (DT(p)[v])z+T(p)w\bigr).
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.9}
\end{equation}

Thus derivatives of \(T\) in the base contribute away from the zero section. The squared normal length in the orthonormal comparison frame is

\begin{equation}
(2z_1+p_1z_2+p_2z_3)^2+
(3z_2+(p_1+p_2)z_3)^2+25z_3^2.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.10}
\end{equation}

The original ball maps to an ellipsoid. Replacing \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.10} by \(\|z\|^2\) would change the given tube. Equations \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:5.1}, \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.2} and \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:6.5} retain \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.8}--\eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:7.10} in the actual ambient move.

\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:supported-move-diagram}
Illustration description: The supported model flow at four exact times, its two intersection signs and full original framing transport.

