{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-PAIR-CREATION-7.2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:pair-creation:7.2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair.md", "source_line": 47067, "source_section": "Creating an original cancelling handle pair", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:pair-creation:7.2}

For the product \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.2}, calculate the derivative of its inverse and verify its height component and level projection.

\textbf{Solution.} The height derivative is \(dF_c\). The level projection \(P(x)=\phi_{a-F_c(x)}x\) has derivative

\begin{equation}
DP=D\phi_{a-F_c(x)}|_x
      -V(P(x))\,dF_c|_x.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:7.5}
\end{equation}

The identity \(F_c\phi_s=F_c+s\), differentiated with \(s\) fixed, gives \(dF_c|_{P(x)}D\phi_s|_x=dF_c|_x\). Since \(dF_c(V)=1\), applying \(dF_c|_{P(x)}\) to \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:7.5} gives zero. Thus \(DP\) lands in the tangent space of the actual \(a\)-level, as required for \(Dj^{-1}\) in \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.5}. Also flow invariance gives \(D\phi_s V(x)=V(P(x))\); therefore \(DP(V(x))=0\). Substituting \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.4} into \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.5} now gives the identity on both the level tangent and height directions. Omitting the second term of \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:7.5} would send \(V(x)\) to the nonzero vector \(V(P(x))\), which is not tangent to the level. The omitted term would make the proposed inverse derivative false.

Illustration description: The exact supported birth path on its axis, the retained incoming ellipse and outward frame, and the full product and original-band maps.

The left panel samples the exact axis restriction \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.7} at zero, the unique birth parameter \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.8}, and one. Its two marked final points have the indices proved in \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-1}. The right panel is the actual incoming coordinate ellipse \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:7.4}, with outward transverse directions from \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:6.7}; the three constant normal directions are outside the plotted slice. The lower arrows are the complete maps \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.2} and \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:5.4}, restricted to the chosen implantation domain for the second arrow. \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-6} prove their inverses, derivatives, support and framing. The plots are numerical illustrations of those exact formulas; no metric identification follows from their appearance.
