{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-LOW-INDEX-SLIDES-2", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:low-index-slides:2", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal.md", "source_line": 52472, "source_section": "Framed handle slides and removal of index one", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:2}

For any group \(G\) and labelled elements \(R_1,\ldots,R_m\), the normal closure of those elements is exactly the set of finite products

\begin{equation}
\prod_{\nu=1}^{N}g_\nu R_{j_\nu}^{\epsilon_\nu}g_\nu^{-1},
 \quad
 N\geq0,\quad g_\nu\in G,\quad
 \epsilon_\nu\in\{-1,1\}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

Proof: the set of such products contains the identity and all the \(R_j\), is closed under multiplication by concatenating the lists, and is closed under inverse by reversing the list and replacing each sign by its negative. Conjugation by \(a\in G\) replaces every \(g_\nu\) by \(ag_\nu\). It is therefore a normal subgroup containing the given elements. Conversely every normal subgroup containing them contains each factor and every finite product. These two inclusions prove \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.1}.

Apply this to the exact group in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9}. For every original one-handle label \(i\), there is a finite identity in the original free group

\begin{equation}
x_i=\prod_{\nu=1}^{N_i}
        g_{i,\nu}R_{j_{i,\nu}}^{\epsilon_{i,\nu}}g_{i,\nu}^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.2}
\end{equation}

There is no assumption that the existing relators themselves can be converted to a free basis. Equation \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.2} is an actual consequence of the proved quotient, and uses no assertion about the Andrews--Curtis problem.

The included \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section}, \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-5} and \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-6}, inserts an index-two/three pair in an original regular band. Use the band immediately above all the old two-handles, and a small coordinate disk in their common exterior, as constructed in \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6} below. Its lower attaching circle bounds the exact framed disk given there, entirely in that exterior. Pulling this disk back to \(N_1\) gives the based element \(R_0=1\) in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.3}. The inserted three-handle has no effect on \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9}, by the index-\(k\geq3\) calculation. It is carried through every subsequent full cobordism comparison.

At the level of based loops, multiplying \(R_0\) by the factors in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.2}, in the order specified in \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-4}, ends with \(x_i\), including every conjugating path and sign. \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-4} construct the required framed slides and prove their multiplication convention.

For a concrete sign check, in the free group on \(x,y\), let \(R_1=xy\) and \(R_2=y\). Then

\begin{equation}
x=R_1R_2^{-1},\qquad y=R_2.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.3}
\end{equation}

The inverse is essential: using two positive copies would give \(xy^2\), not \(x\). With a connecting path represented by a further word \(g\), the corresponding identity is

\begin{equation}
gxg^{-1}=(gR_1g^{-1})(gR_2^{-1}g^{-1}).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.4}
\end{equation}

The middle \(g^{-1}g\) cancels as based path concatenation, so both copies use the same actual connecting-path class. The later geometric slide must preserve that distinction when choosing its band.
