We now apply all these constructions on the original cobordism. Keep the simultaneous old two-handle stage of \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6}. In a regular band above \(b\) and below the first old higher-index critical value, or below the outgoing collar if there is no such point, insert the supported two/three pair. Use a small coordinate disk in \(H(e(E))\) for its projection to the old level. The full compact support and the entire lower attaching disk of the pair fit in that coordinate product by the explicit positive scales of \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-5}. Its \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-6} computes the disk and its full extended frame. Denote the new index-two point by \(p\) and its paired index-three point by \(r\). Their values satisfy

\begin{equation}
c_2<b<f(p)<f(r).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Use a slightly smaller regular band below \(p\) to record the complete attaching tube pulled down to \(f=b\), and then through \(H^{-1}\). Call it

\begin{equation}
\alpha_0:S^1_{a_0}\times D^{n-2}_{b_0}\longrightarrow S.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

The choice of insertion disk puts its whole image in \(e(E)\), and its core bounds the specified disk there. Its word pulled to \(N\) is therefore the exact identity \(R_0=1\).

Choose a finite normal-closure expression for the retained class \(u_q\) of \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.6}:

\begin{equation}
u_q=\prod_{\nu=1}^{N_q}
           g_\nu R_{j_\nu}^{\epsilon_\nu}g_\nu^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.3}
\end{equation}

Existence follows from the exact quotient \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9}, since \(C\) is simply connected. Its factors, order, whiskers and signs are all retained. Equation \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.3} does not require replacing \(u_q\) by a preferred free generator.

Perform the slides of \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-4} on the simultaneous boundary \(S\), using the factors in reverse order and the signs \(\sigma_\nu=-\epsilon_\nu\). Here is why the simultaneous use changes no part of those proofs. The positive face of target \(j\), including its crossing box, is the same original face in the disjoint union of handles. Use the simultaneous \(P\) for the path transport. Its complement is connected and induces the same based paths in \(N\). All paths can avoid the other whole attaching collars: in each punctured normal tube the radial homotopy

\begin{equation}
(\theta,z)\longmapsto
 \left(\theta,
     \frac{(1-t)\|z\|+t\max(\|z\|,R_j)}{\|z\|}z\right),
 \qquad z\ne0,
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

pushes them outside a retained slightly extended radius \(R_j>s_j\), and is the identity outside that radius. The disjoint extended tubes make these homotopies compatible. The original radius, the new chosen \(R_j\), and the entire homotopy appear in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.4}. Smooth the path relative its endpoint germs afterwards. The added collars still miss the chosen basepoint. A generic small relative path perturbation avoids the moving circle and all remaining cores. Consequently the supported transport and crossing in target \(j\) miss every other handle face and belt. After each crossing, compress the complete moving tube off the belts and use the disjoint outward radial flows to put it back in \(e(E)\). These flows fix all original cores and preserve the based word just computed. Their exact derivatives and collar extensions are those of \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-3}.

Thus the finite composite is an actual ambient isotopy of \(S\), and the final core pulled through \(P\) has based class \(u_q\). The fixed old target cores still have their original classes \(R_j\). By \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:5.5}, that core and \(\kappa\) are freely homotopic in the complement of all \(L_j\). Apply \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-5}, lift by \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:5.15}, and concatenate with the slides. Compress the final full moving tube around \(\kappa\), within its exterior neighbourhood. With smooth time cutoffs at all joins this gives one ambient isotopy \(\mathcal A_t\) of \(S\) with

\begin{equation}
\mathcal A_0=1,\qquad
 \mathcal A_1\alpha_0=e\widetilde\alpha,\qquad
 \widetilde\alpha(\theta,0)=\kappa(\theta),\qquad
 \widetilde\alpha
   (S^1_{a_0}\times D^{n-2}_{b_0})\subset E.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.5}
\end{equation}

The final frame is the actual transported frame in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:5.14}. No extension of an arbitrary sphere diffeomorphism, and no assumed cancellation of a frame twist, was used.
