{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ADAMS-TOWER-5", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:adams-tower:5", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits.md", "source_line": 59621, "source_section": "The Adams tower and exact inverse limits", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:adams-tower:5}

Use the original Hopf algebra, full coproduct and word differential of the included degree-six cobar proof (\cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section}). \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-5} prove the finite filtration in each internal degree and the full calculation over \(R=\mathbb F_2[x]\). We retain its dictionary

\begin{equation}
(x,y,a,z,b,c,w)=(h_{10},h_{11},h_{20},h_{12},h_{21},h_{30},h_{13}),
 \qquad B=a^2,
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

with all original \((s,t,W)\) degrees as in its \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.1}. In this section \(d_r^{\mathrm M}\) denotes the differential of that weight filtration. This superscript keeps it distinct from the tower differential \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:3.3}.

The existing proof gives

\begin{equation}
(E_2^{\mathrm M})_{t-s=5}=(R/(x))\{y^5,yB\},
 \qquad (E_2^{\mathrm M})_{t-s=6}
       =(R/(x))\{y^6,y^2B\}\oplus R\{z^2\}.
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

We supply the adjacent calculation needed to determine the outgoing differential of \(yB\). The complete \(R\)-basis at difference four is
\(y^4,y^2a,a^2,yz\).
This follows by solving the same nonnegative exponent equation used in the cited proof: \(e_y+2e_a+3e_z=4\), with every higher generator too large and every power of \(x\) retained in \(R\). Its only nonzero differential is
\(d_1^{\mathrm M}(y^2a)=xy^3\).
The complete difference-five basis in the cited \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.4} has images generating
\(R\{xy^4,yz\}\).
Comparison of coefficients in the polynomial ring therefore gives

\begin{equation}
\ker(d_1^{\mathrm M}|_4)=R\{y^4,B,yz\},\qquad
 \operatorname{im}(d_1^{\mathrm M}|_5)=R\{xy^4,yz\},\qquad
 (E_2^{\mathrm M})_{t-s=4}=(R/(x))\{y^4\}\oplus R\{B\}.
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.3}
\end{equation}

In particular \(y^4\) is nonzero. There is no \(d_2^{\mathrm M}\) by the proved parity relation \(W\equiv s\pmod2\).

The complete seven-term original cochain \(\mathcal B\) of the cited \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.3} has the full differential

\begin{equation}
d\mathcal B=[\zeta_1^2|\zeta_1^2|\zeta_1^2]
                   +[\zeta_1|\zeta_1|\zeta_1^4],
 \qquad d_3^{\mathrm M}B=y^3+x^2z.
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.4}
\end{equation}

Both terms, including the second contribution, are retained. Since \(y,z\) are represented by actual primitive cocycles and \(yz=0\) on \(E_2^{\mathrm M}\), the Leibniz rule gives

\begin{equation}
d_3^{\mathrm M}(yB)=y^4+x^2yz=y^4\ne0,
 \qquad d_3^{\mathrm M}(y^2B)=y^5+x^2y^2z=y^5.
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.5}
\end{equation}

The first differential removes \(yB\); the second makes \(y^5\) a boundary. These are every generator in \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.2}, and each is already killed by \(x\). Thus the entire difference-five part of \(E_4^{\mathrm M}\) is zero. Later pages are subquotients in each position, so they remain zero. The complete finite-filtration argument of the cited \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-2} now proves

\begin{equation}
H^{s,s+5}(C^\bullet(H_{\mathrm{cl}}))=0
                 \quad\text{for every }s\geq0.
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.6}
\end{equation}

This is an all-length theorem over the original algebra, not a finite sampling of the cobar complex.

The use of \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.6} in the topological calculation is exact. The degree-six class has \((s,t)=(2,8)\). A tower differential leaving it has target
\((s,t)=(2+r,7+r)\), whose difference is five. A differential entering it has source \((2-r,9-r)\); for \(r>2\) the filtration is negative, and for \(r=2\) it is \((0,7)\), where the length-zero cobar group is zero. These index calculations identify every possible differential. Applying them to the actual tower awaits its proved cobar identification; no algebra-to-topology substitution is implicit in \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:5.6}.
