{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ADAMS-TOWER-6", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:adams-tower:6", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits.md", "source_line": 59692, "source_section": "The Adams tower and exact inverse limits", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:adams-tower:6}

The original TeX of Dugger--Isaksen, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.1632v1}{arXiv:0901.1632v1}, lines 2218--2452, constructs the smash-fibre tower and compares its resolutions. Its general-\(X\) lemma at lines 2362--2374 writes an Ext target with \(\mathbb M_2\) twice, while the displayed resolution begins with \(H_{**}(X)\). Applying the stated comodule Hom functor to that resolution gives \(\operatorname{Ext}_{H_{**}H}(\mathbb M_2,H_{**}(X))\). This retains the actual resolved comodule. For the sphere the latter is \(\mathbb M_2\), so that specialization is unchanged.

The original TeX of Goerss, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1006v1}{arXiv:0802.1006v1}, \cite[Section~1]{mapsGoerss2008}, gives the cohomological tower. Its initial map at lines 98--102 has the correct variance. The display at lines 133--139 reverses the two cohomology modules. For a map \(f:X\to Y\), the actual map is

\begin{equation}
f^*:H^*(Y;\mathbb F_2)\longrightarrow H^*(X;\mathbb F_2),
 \qquad [u:Y\to\Sigma^qH]\longmapsto[u\circ f].
 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

For a stable operation represented by \(\theta:H\to\Sigma^aH\), its value on \([u]\) is \([(\Sigma^q\theta)\circ u]\). Associativity of composition proves
\(f^*\theta[u]=\theta f^*[u]\), including the displayed suspension. Therefore \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:6.1} is the required module homomorphism; its direction cannot be reversed by renaming its source and target.

The vanishing-line display in that same source, lines 469--490, also cannot be imported as written. It asserts vanishing when \(0<t-s<2s+\epsilon\), with \(\epsilon=1\) for \(s=1\). At \((s,t)=(1,2)\), it would assert zero. The source's own lines 440--459 instead assert a nonzero group there. In the original cobar algebra this nonzero class has a direct proof: \([\zeta_1^2]\) is a cocycle because \(\zeta_1^2\) is primitive, and no positive-internal-degree length-zero cochain exists to have it as a boundary. Thus the displayed vanishing inequality fails at this precise bidegree. We do not assume a repaired general inequality. \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-4} give the complete finite-tower convergence argument, and \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-5} supplies the specific adjacent vanishing calculation needed for the degree-six class.

The source files and versions are retained in the private reading ledger. Actual reading for Goerss is stras1.tex lines 1--601, macros.tex in full and bibliostras.tex in full. No claim of reading its later sections is made. The source's omitted comparison and finiteness arguments have not been promoted to proved facts about the original sphere.
