{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ACTUAL-DEGREE8-4", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:actual-degree8:4", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight.md", "source_line": 55786, "source_section": "The actual mod-two cooperation comparison through degree eight", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:actual-degree8:4}

Let \(L_q=|K^q|\) be the original simplicial-cocycle spaces of the Adams-tower companion. Their actual abelian group operations, full cochain bundles and positive suspension maps were proved there. In particular \(L_0=k\), and \(L_q\) has its only nonzero homotopy group \(k\) in degree \(q\).

We first compare the classifying group of \(K^q\) with the original \(K^{q+1}\). The homogeneous bisimplicial object has entries \((K^q_t)^{s+1}\), horizontal faces deleting an entry, and quotient by the diagonal group action \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:2.3}. On diagonals it gives a degreewise exact sequence of simplicial abelian groups

\begin{equation}
0\longrightarrow K^q\longrightarrow E K^q\longrightarrow B K^q\longrightarrow0.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

The middle realization is contractible. In the horizontal direction, inserting the zero vertex contracts the homogeneous bar construction. This insertion commutes with every vertical map, so gives a contraction of the iterated realization. The diagonal-realization comparison proved in \cref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:section-2} carries it to a contraction of \(|EK^q|\). The actual quotient-bundle argument in the Adams-tower companion, \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-9}, applies to the exact sequence \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.1}, including its local sections and relative disk lifting. Its homotopy exact sequence proves that \(|BK^q|\) has its sole nonzero homotopy group \(k\) in degree \(q+1\).

The cohomology-representing construction already proved for the original \(L_{q+1}\) now gives a map

\begin{equation}
j_q:|BK^q|\longrightarrow L_{q+1}
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.2}
\end{equation}

which sends the generator specified by the connecting map of \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.1} to the original generator. To construct it, choose a cellular degree-\(q+1\) cocycle representing the homomorphism on the first homology group. Make the \(q\)-skeleton constant and assign that cocycle value to each oriented \((q+1)\)-cell. On each \((q+2)\)-cell, the attaching obstruction is the signed sum of those values on its boundary, which is zero by the cocycle equation. Thus the map extends across that dimension. All subsequent attaching obstructions lie in the higher, zero homotopy groups of \(L_{q+1}\), so the map extends across every remaining cell. This is the original cellular cohomology-representation construction with its first obstruction retained. The cellular Hurewicz comparison gives the required generator in degree \(q+1\); for \(q=0\), degree-one cohomology is directly the homomorphism on \(\pi_1\). The map is an isomorphism on every homotopy group and hence a homotopy equivalence by the course's CW argument.

It also preserves group addition up to homotopy. The two maps from \(|BK^q|\times|BK^q|\) to \(L_{q+1}\) in that assertion are classified by degree-\(q+1\) cohomology. There are no mixed terms in that degree: each factor is \(q\)-connected, and the field cross product makes the first possible mixed positive degree \(2q+2\). Their restrictions to either factor coincide with \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.2}, so their cohomology classes, and therefore their homotopy classes, coincide. This proves that \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.2} preserves the actual Pontryagin homology algebra.

Finally, \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.2} carries the classifying-space suspension to the original positive suspension \(\sigma_q:\Sigma L_q\to L_{q+1}\). Their adjoint loops have the same connecting value by the endpoint calculation after \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:2.9} and the original endpoint calculation \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:9.6} of the Adams-tower chapter. Maps from \(\Sigma L_q\) to \(L_{q+1}\) are classified by \(\widetilde H^{q+1}(\Sigma L_q;k)=\widetilde H^q(L_q;k)=k\), including \(q=0\). Their common positive generator thus proves the full homotopy of these maps, not merely agreement on one homotopy group. This gives the receiving algebra and suspension comparison for the original coefficient spectrum.

At \(q=1\), the projective chapter's original bar cell \(\alpha_n=[g|\cdots|g]\) has boundary zero in \(k\). The Pontryagin shuffle gives the entire product

\begin{equation}
\alpha_a\ast\alpha_b=\left(\binom{a+b}{a}\bmod2\right)\alpha_{a+b}.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.3}
\end{equation}

Each shuffle has the same image word: at each coordinate exactly one input contributes \(g\), while the other contributes the identity. This identifies the algebra as divided powers on \(\alpha_1\), or as the exterior algebra on \(\alpha_{2^j}\), with their original degrees \(2^j\).

Inductively, the full homology algebra of \(L_q\), for \(q\geq1\), is exterior on generators indexed by

\begin{equation}
J=(j_1,\ldots,j_q),\qquad
0\leq j_1\leq\cdots\leq j_q,
\qquad |c_J|=\sum_{i=1}^q2^{j_i}.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.4}
\end{equation}

The base is \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.3}. Given the assertion for \(q\), the actual algebra map of \cref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:section-3}, followed by \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:2.9} and \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.2}, proves the assertion for \(q+1\). More explicitly, the divided-power generator of index \(2^j\) on \(\sigma c_J\) has degree

\begin{equation}
2^j\left(1+\sum_{i=1}^q2^{j_i}\right)
 =2^j+\sum_{i=1}^q2^{j+j_i}.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.5}
\end{equation}

Its new ordered index is \((j,j+j_1,\ldots,j+j_q)\). This correspondence is bijective: recover \(j\) as the first entry and subtract it from every subsequent entry to recover \(J\). Thus no generator is lost or counted twice. The original suspension satisfies

\begin{equation}
\sigma_*c_J=c_{(0,J)},
\qquad
\ker\sigma_*=(H_{>0}(L_q;k))^{\ast2},
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.6}
\end{equation}

where the right side means the span of products of two positive classes, not only literal squares. The image in \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.6} is an independent family of indecomposables. This is the actual suspension map, by the comparison already proved, and holds at every stage.
