{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ACTUAL-DEGREE8-5", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:actual-degree8:5", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight.md", "source_line": 55852, "source_section": "The actual mod-two cooperation comparison through degree eight", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:actual-degree8:5}

The existing stable homology comparison identifies the original cooperation space as

\begin{equation}
\Gamma_r=H_r(H;k)=\operatorname*{colim}_{q}\widetilde H_{q+r}(L_q;k)
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

along the positive original suspension maps. In \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.4}, the smallest generator degree is \(q\), occurring only for the all-zero index. Its square is zero, and the next generator degree is \(q+1\). Therefore a product of two distinct positive generators has degree at least \(2q+1\). For \(q\geq\max(1,r)\), every positive class of degree \(q+r\) is consequently a linear combination of the individual generators \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.4}. Their indexing equation is exactly

\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^q(2^{j_i}-1)=r.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

Each positive entry contributes at least one, so there are at most \(r\) positive entries. For the stated \(q\), all multisets of positive entries satisfying \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.2} fit, and zeros supply the remaining entries. Suspension inserts precisely one initial zero and is a bijection on these bases. Thus the stable dimension is the number of original monomials of weighted degree \(r\) in \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:0.1}. In particular the full dimensions for \(0\leq r\leq8\) are

\begin{equation}
(\dim\Gamma_0,\ldots,\dim\Gamma_8)
 =(1,1,1,2,2,2,3,4,4).
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.3}
\end{equation}

This proves an actual vector-space calculation. The identification of its products with those of \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:0.1} will use the already constructed map, not this count alone.
