{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ACTUAL-DEGREE8-6", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:actual-degree8:6", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight.md", "source_line": 55881, "source_section": "The actual mod-two cooperation comparison through degree eight", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:actual-degree8:6}

Put \(v=\Phi(\xi_1)\). The projective chapter proved
\(\langle\beta,v\rangle=1\) and that \(\Gamma_1\) is one-dimensional, where \(\beta\) is the connecting operation for the original sequence \(0\to k\xrightarrow{1\mapsto2}\mathbb Z/4\to k\to0\). Its original projective formula is \(\beta(x)=x^2\).

The stabilization of \(\alpha_2\in H_2(L_1;k)\), dual to \(x^2\), is exactly \(v\). Indeed pairing its stable image with \(\beta\) evaluates the representing operation on the original universal class \(x\); the result is \(\langle x^2,\alpha_2\rangle=1\). This pairing is the actual multiplication map used in projective equation \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.3}, since composing its right unit with the operation is that operation. Both nonzero classes lie in the one-dimensional original \(\Gamma_1\), so they coincide.

For \(n\geq1\), let \(u_n\in H^n(L_n;k)\) be the original universal class, and let

\begin{equation}
m_n:L_1^{\times n}\longrightarrow L_n
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

represent the cup product of the \(n\) original degree-one classes. It is the level-space map of the original spectrum multiplication: that multiplication was compared with the original cup product by its unit and degree-zero coefficient map in the cooperation chapter. Define the actual class

\begin{equation}
z_n=(m_n)_*(\alpha_2\times\cdots\times\alpha_2)
 \in H_{2n}(L_n;k).
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.2}
\end{equation}

The full pullback is \(m_n^*u_n=x_1\cdots x_n\). Retaining the integer interchange sign before reducing coefficients gives

\begin{equation}
m_n^*(u_n^2)=(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}x_1^2\cdots x_n^2,
\qquad \langle u_n^2,z_n\rangle=1\in k.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.3}
\end{equation}

The evaluation of the exterior product is one on the original product of the \(\alpha_2\); its coefficient sign has image one in \(k\). In particular \(z_n\neq0\).

This detected class is indecomposable for the Pontryagin product. One can see this from \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.4}: its degree is \(2n\), while every product of two distinct positive generators has degree at least \(2n+1\), and the repeated bottom generator has square zero. There is also a direct comparison with the detecting functional. The original universal class is primitive under group addition, since that addition represents addition of cohomology classes. Therefore \(u_n^2\) is primitive as well, by the full characteristic-two square expansion. Such a class evaluates to zero on a product of two positive homology classes: both terms of its coproduct have a degree-zero factor. Thus \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.3} independently excludes decomposability of \(z_n\).

Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.6} now proves that \(z_n\) and each of its subsequent suspensions are nonzero indecomposable classes. Its image in \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:5.1} is therefore nonzero. That image is exactly \(v^n\), by \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.2} and the original stable cross-product comparison. In particular its iterated desuspension sign from \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.2a} is

\begin{equation}
(-1)^{\sum_{j=1}^n2(j-1)}=(-1)^{n(n-1)}=1;
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.4}
\end{equation}

all original input degrees are two and every desuspension has degree one. We have proved the all-exponent statement

\begin{equation}
v^n\neq0\quad\hbox{in }\Gamma_n\qquad(n\geq1).
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.5}
\end{equation}

This is a calculation of the product from the original spectrum multiplication. It is distinct from the Pontryagin product \(\ast\), whose role was to determine the exact suspension kernel; the maps \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:6.4} prove their required relation.
