{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ACTUAL-DEGREE8-8", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:actual-degree8:8", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight.md", "source_line": 56077, "source_section": "The actual mod-two cooperation comparison through degree eight", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical proved construction", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:actual-degree8:8}

At fixed cobar length \(s\), the full degree-six cohomology calculation has the three adjacent terms of total degree \(s+6\), at lengths \(s-1,s,s+1\). Their largest possible single-letter degrees are respectively eight, seven and six, because every other letter has positive degree. These are the original all-length bounds already proved in the actual-cooperation companion. Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:7.3} therefore identifies every factor in all three terms. Applying \(\Phi\) to each letter is the actual cobar chain map already constructed in the projective companion; its full differential agrees term by term because the full coproduct and both coaugmentation endpoints agree. We obtain the actual isomorphism

\begin{equation}
H^{s,s+6}(C^*(A))\xrightarrow{\cong}
H^{s,s+6}(C^*(\Gamma))
\quad\hbox{for every }s\geq0.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:8.1}
\end{equation}

For lengths zero or one, empty or negative adjacent terms have their original meanings: the empty word has degree zero and a negative length contributes zero. The same reasoning, with bounds seven, six and five, gives the entire degree-five comparison. No bounded-length truncation has been used.

The \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:six-cobar-all-lengths} has one degree-six class, at bidegree \((2,8)\), represented by \([\xi_1^4|\xi_1^4]\), and has zero degree-five cohomology at every length. The actual Adams-resolution comparison hence gives these statements for the original mod-two Adams \(E_2\)-page. Its sole degree-six class is represented by

\begin{equation}
[(\xi_1^{\rm act})^4|(\xi_1^{\rm act})^4].
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:8.2}
\end{equation}

It is a permanent cycle. Every outgoing differential lands in the degree-five line, which is zero from the second page onward. An incoming \(d_r\), with \(r\geq2\), would start at \((2-r,9-r)\). At \(r=2\), this is \((0,7)\), whose group is zero because length zero contains only the degree-zero empty word; for \(r>2\) the filtration is negative. Thus no incoming differential exists.

The complete original convergence and arithmetic comparison already proved in the course now identify

\begin{equation}
\pi_6(\mathbb S^{\wedge}_2)\cong k,
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:8.3}
\end{equation}

with its sole nonzero associated graded piece at filtration two, and with the representative \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:8.2}. To check the extension assertion, let \(F^s\) be that complete separated Adams filtration. Every successive quotient is zero except \(F^2/F^3=k\). Thus \(F^0=F^1=F^2\), and \(F^3=F^4=\cdots\). Separatedness makes their intersection, and hence \(F^3\), zero. This leaves exactly the single quotient \(k\), with no additive extension. The equality with the original arithmetic completion uses the previously proved unit-preserving map; no unproved equality of integral and completed homotopy groups is inferred here.

The \cref{courseIII:the-original-odd-prime-cooperations-and-degree-six-adams-calculation:odd-actual-adams} now proves the full signed bar and original suspension calculation, the actual cooperation degrees needed by stem six, and the signed comparison with the original Adams tower. It proves \(\pi_6(\mathbb S^{\wedge}_p)=0\) for every odd prime. The \cref{courseIII:the-original-integral-stable-group-and-its-completion-maps:integral-degree-six-answer} now proves finite generation of the original sphere groups, the exact suspension range and the full unit comparison with all prime completions. It proves \(\pi_6(\mathbb S)=\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z\) and that the original 2-completion unit is an isomorphism in degree six. The \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-original-nullbordism} constructs the full quadratic invariant, proves framed bordism invariance and gives value zero for the original framed homotopy six-sphere, with an actual framed nullbordism. The \cref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map} now constructs the new interior gluing with its full frame defect, cancels every actual handle and gives the orientation-preserving diffeomorphism to the original six-sphere, homotopic to its original degree-one map. In particular this chapter does not yet identify the original manifold's image in the two-element group \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:8.3}. It supplies its exact actual target and permanent-cycle representative.
