{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-ACTUAL-DEGREE8-9.1", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:actual-degree8:9.1", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight.md", "source_line": 56118, "source_section": "The actual mod-two cooperation comparison through degree eight", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical worked example", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:actual-degree8:9.1}

Find the complete homology basis of \(L_2\) in degrees two through six, and calculate the suspension on every class in those bases. Exhibit a nonzero class whose suspension is zero.

\textbf{Solution.} The tuples in \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.4} give the generators

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
a&=c_{(0,0)},\quad b=c_{(0,1)},\quad c=c_{(1,1)},\\
d&=c_{(0,2)},\quad e=c_{(1,2)},\\
|a|&=2,\quad |b|=3,\quad |c|=4,\quad |d|=5,\quad |e|=6.
\end{aligned}
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:9.1}
\end{equation}

There are no other generators in these degrees: the only powers of two at most six are one, two and four, and the displayed pairs are exactly those whose sums are at most six. The algebra is exterior, so every distinct-generator product occurs once and every repeated-generator product is zero. Its full bases are

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
r&2&3&4&5&6\\\hline
H_r(L_2;k)&k\{a\}&k\{b\}&k\{c\}&k\{d,a\ast b\}&k\{e,a\ast c\}.
\end{array}
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:9.2}
\end{equation}

Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:4.6}, with the original positive suspension, sends the five listed generators respectively to

\begin{equation}
c_{(0,0,0)},\quad c_{(0,0,1)},\quad c_{(0,1,1)},\quad
c_{(0,0,2)},\quad c_{(0,1,2)}.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:9.3}
\end{equation}

Each target is a nonzero indecomposable. Both products in \eqref{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:9.2} have zero suspension. Explicitly, in the bar of the chosen exterior chain model,

\begin{equation}
d[a|b]=(-1)^{2+1}[a\ast b]=[a\ast b],\qquad
d[a|c]=(-1)^{2+1}[a\ast c]=[a\ast c]
\quad\hbox{over }k.
\label{courseIII:the-actual-mod-two-cooperation-comparison-through-degree-eight:eq:9.4}
\end{equation}

The internal terms vanish because these inputs are cycles; both augmentation endpoints vanish because the inputs are positive. The actual bar comparison takes these equations to bounding chains for the suspended original products. Thus \(a\ast b\neq0\) is the requested nonzero class with zero suspension. The calculation also shows why the proof uses the indecomposable quotient before passing to the stable system.
