{"row_key": "WORKING_EDITION_COURSE_PROVIDER:CG-S6-MILNOR-COBAR-1.1", "source_compendium_paper": "III", "source_compendium_label": "courseIII:milnor-cobar:1.1", "source_file": "sources/codex_workspace/courses/CG-S6/src/the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation.md", "source_line": 56898, "source_section": "The degree-six calculation in the full Milnor cobar complex", "placement_basis": "plan §3 classical/course material", "placement_state": "provisional editorial mapping; no current mathematical validation inferred", "source_kind": "Classical theorem", "source_statement": "\label{courseIII:milnor-cobar:1.1}

Work first over \(\mathbb F _2\). Define

\begin{equation}
H=\mathbb F _2[\zeta _1,\zeta _2,\ldots],\qquad
 |\zeta _i|=2^i-1,\qquad \zeta _0=1,
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

with counit \(\epsilon(\zeta_i)=0\) for \(i>0\), and with the multiplicative coproduct

\begin{equation}
\Delta\zeta_i
 =\sum_{k=0}^{i}\zeta_{i-k}^{\,2^k}\otimes\zeta_k
 =\zeta_i\otimes1+1\otimes\zeta_i+
   \sum_{k=1}^{i-1}\zeta_{i-k}^{\,2^k}\otimes\zeta_k .
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.2}
\end{equation}

Both endpoint terms in the first sum are displayed in the second expression. The counit identities follow from them. Coassociativity follows on each generator because both iterated coproducts are the same full sum

\begin{equation}
\sum_{\substack{a,b,c\geq0\\a+b+c=i}}
 \zeta_a^{\,2^{b+c}}\otimes
 \zeta_b^{\,2^c}\otimes\zeta_c .
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.3}
\end{equation}

Multiplicativity then proves it on every polynomial. No restriction to the first few generators is made in the definition.

Put \(\overline H=\ker\epsilon\). A word of length \(s\) and internal degree \(t\) is denoted by

\begin{equation}
[m_1|\cdots|m_s]\in C^{s,t}(H)
   =(\overline H^{\otimes s})_t,\qquad
 \sum_{\nu=1}^{s}|m_\nu|=t .
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.4}
\end{equation}

Here \(C^{0,0}=\mathbb F _2\), and the other length-zero components are zero. Write

\begin{equation}
\overline\Delta m=\Delta m-m\otimes1-1\otimes m.
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.5}
\end{equation}

The two removed terms in \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.5} are exactly the displayed counit terms of the full coproduct. The differential is

\begin{equation}
d[m_1|\cdots|m_s]
 =\sum_{\nu=1}^{s}\ \sum_{\overline\Delta m_\nu
                       =\sum a\otimes b}
 [m_1|\cdots|m_{\nu-1}|a|b|m_{\nu+1}|\cdots|m_s].
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.6}
\end{equation}

All coefficients are in \(\mathbb F _2\). It has bidegree \((1,0)\); hence it decreases the difference \(t-s\) by one.

The equality \(d^2=0\) follows directly. Splittings at two different positions occur in both possible orders and cancel. The two orders of splitting one position agree by \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.3} and likewise cancel. Concatenation makes this a differential algebra. We will calculate

\begin{equation}
\mathcal H^{s,t}(H)
  =\frac{\ker(d:C^{s,t}\longrightarrow C^{s+1,t})}
         {\operatorname{im}(d:C^{s-1,t}\longrightarrow C^{s,t})}.
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.7}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}
\label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:source-theorem-1.1}
For the full Hopf algebra \eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.2},

\begin{equation}
\mathcal H^{s,s+6}(H)=
 \begin{cases}
 \mathbb F _2\{[\zeta_1^4|\zeta_1^4]\},&s=2,\\
 0,&s\ne2.
 \end{cases}
 \label{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.8}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\Uses{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.1}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.2}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:2.4}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.7}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:2.2}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:2.7}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:2.8}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.6}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.1}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:1.6}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.2}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.4}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.8}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.3}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.7}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.6}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:4.10}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.8}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-2}; \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:eq:3.7}}


In particular the assertion includes arbitrarily large \(s\). The proof occupies \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-2} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-3} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-4} and \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-5}. \cref{courseIII:the-degree-six-milnor-cobar-calculation:section-6} proves the corresponding vanishing at every odd prime.


